WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.367
- Can I bring the public
board into session

00:00:06.367 --> 00:00:08.700
and welcome everybody.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.640
We have apologies from Simon Wessely,

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.800
Jacqui Rock, Rakesh Kapoor,

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.500
and also from three of our colleagues

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.070
who were with us this
morning in the private board,

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:23.910
Jeremy Townsend and Andrew
Morris and Susan Kilsby.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.383
Can I ask if anybody has an-

00:00:29.303 --> 00:00:30.510
And we have technology with us.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.360
That's all right.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.710
Can I ask if anyone has
any interest to declare

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.360
any arising interests since
the last board meeting?

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:39.713
Thank you very much.

00:00:39.713 --> 00:00:43.080
And can I ask the board for
their approval of the minutes

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.903
for the meeting held on the 27th of July?

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.270
Thank you very much. So I'll adopt those.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:53.340
Let me just give some reflections

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.220
at the start of the meeting as chair

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.510
and in starting that,

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.030
I'd just like to thank colleagues actually

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:03.340
for all their work
outside the board meetings

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.330
and in various committees

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.960
as well as engagement across the NHS.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.870
So actually since the
last public board meeting,

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:16.167
we've had two strategy board meetings

00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:20.100
and also a board deep dive session.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.923
And on the committee work,

00:01:21.923 --> 00:01:23.280
there've been three people

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.050
and remuneration committee meetings,

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:28.350
one Data Digital and Technology committee,

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.540
one Workforce Training
and Education committee,

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.600
two new NHS England committees,

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.350
one Audit and Risk committee,

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.630
and one Quality of
Patient Safety committee.

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.840
So I thank my colleagues
for all their endeavours

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.463
in those meetings.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:46.500
I should start though,

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.690
I think on the topic of industrial action,

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.420
which clearly is continuing
to cause huge disruption

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.410
across our services.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.170
And this week has been
particularly difficult

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:56.580
for colleagues across the NHS.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.040
It has felt very pressured.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.160
And while Christmas day
cover is being provided,

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.770
we aren't seeing Christmas
day levels of demand.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.783
It's clearly much higher.

00:02:07.676 --> 00:02:10.170
And the NHS has now seen
our hospital doctors

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:13.620
undertake 720 hours of industrial action

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:14.940
in total since March,

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.210
equivalent to facing a full calendar month

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:22.140
without a significant proportion
of our medical workforce.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.840
Now our staff are doing
everything they possibly can

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.070
to keep services going,

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:29.790
but it is simply not sustainable
to continue to operate

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.130
with this amount of disruption.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.420
And last month we saw the number

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.220
of routine appointments postponed,

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.100
ensuring the NHS could continue
providing emergency care

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.880
during strike action, surpass 1 million.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.860
We know this is an underestimate of impact

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:47.253
because hospitals aren't
scheduling unknown strike days.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.470
Now behind every,

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:50.910
and this is a really important point,

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:54.450
that behind every rearranged
appointment or procedure

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:59.250
is a patient experiencing
added distress or anxiety

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:03.870
or pain or deterioration
potentially in their condition.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.240
And it's not just rescheduled
appointments and operations.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.910
There is also the
financial impact for trust.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.000
There is pressure in
urgent and emergency care

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.680
and there is the administrative burden

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.530
of setting up and standing down work

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.830
and the continuous sorting out of rotors.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.690
And I've heard this week about problems

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:24.060
it is having on discharging patients

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.470
and there are simply not enough staff

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:29.670
on Christmas day cover days
to complete the usual tasks

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:33.840
while also dealing with business
as usual levels of demand.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.790
And it will be impossible, I think,

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:38.670
to manage this coming
into the winter period

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.260
with flu and Covid

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.840
and other respiratory
illnesses circulating

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.070
and adding significant further
pressure to our services.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:48.990
But we will continue, of course,

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.510
to do all we can to keep
caring for patients,

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:52.980
putting them first,

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.880
but we urgently need to see
a clear path to resolution

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.980
and for all parties to work together

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.170
to do the right thing by patients

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.903
and to find an agreement to this dispute.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.080
I know Amanda will also comment

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.203
in her Chief Executive reports
upon industrial action.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:12.870
It's important to recognise

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.960
that since we last met
as a board in public,

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.700
the Lucy Letby situation,

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.470
and actually again everyone would've seen

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:21.540
that the Cheshire
Constabulary has launched

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.820
a corporate manslaughter investigation

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.520
following Lucy Letby's convictions.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.830
The investigation is in
its very early stages

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:31.530
and I know the trust will
continue to work closely

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:32.940
with the police on this

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.160
and we will also cooperate fully

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.313
with the statutory inquiry
announced by the government.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.880
It's important that we as
a board continue to discuss

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:43.530
what actions we are taking

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.870
so that we are doing
everything within our powers

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:49.200
to prevent anything like
this ever happening again.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:50.760
And while these appalling crimes

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.890
were the actions of a single individual,

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:55.200
the case is also a stark reminder

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.970
of the importance of NHS leaders

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:58.920
listening and acting appropriately

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.430
to the concerns of patients
or families or staff

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:06.240
following whistle-blowing
procedures and good governance.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:07.110
We've done a number of things.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.280
We've strengthened, as you know,

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:09.810
the fit and proper persons test

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.600
by bringing in additional
background checks

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:15.000
and ensuring the
director's fitness to lead

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.773
is added to their staff record.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:20.430
All organisations providing NHS services

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:23.340
are expected to adopt the updated strength

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.590
in Freedom to Speak Up policy

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:28.170
by January 24 at the latest.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.810
And we've also asked our NHS
leaders to ensure that staff

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.570
have easy access on how to speak up,

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.910
and we've asked boards to report regularly

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:39.810
to review and to act upon
their available data.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:41.400
And clearly we've been having a series

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.280
of very important conversations
with our various partners

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.383
on the topic of regulation.

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:53.870
Moving on to NHS England and
what's called the restructure.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:58.500
And my thanks here to to Wol Kolade

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.237
who's chaired the new
NHS England Committee,

00:06:00.237 --> 00:06:03.030
and to Steve Russell who's
leading this exercise

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:04.200
as an executive,

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.850
but also my thanks to the executives

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.273
for actually dealing with what is

00:06:07.273 --> 00:06:10.440
a very, very significant transformation.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.750
So as we continue to focus on

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:15.000
the organisational
restructure of NHS England,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.606
having merged with NHS Digital

00:06:17.606 --> 00:06:20.490
and Health Education England,

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.280
we're now on track to
deliver an approximate

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:27.420
35% reduction in our headcount,

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:31.980
or over 8,500 working time equivalents.

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:34.710
It does project a saving
of over 400 million pounds,

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.477
which will go to the front line

00:06:36.477 --> 00:06:38.520
and is equivalent, essentially,

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:42.150
to funding around 8,000 nurses.

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:43.860
In saying that,

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.810
we absolutely recognise
it's a difficult time

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:48.780
for many of our colleagues
here and also in the regions.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.580
But the important thing
is it will allow us

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:53.970
to reduce bureaucracy,
to de-bureaucratize,

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.790
and to focus on greater
devolution to the ICBs,

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:59.910
enabling the ICBs to
continue to step forward

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:02.580
into the very significant
role that they have

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:03.933
within our ecosystem.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:06.293
Moving on from that,

00:07:06.293 --> 00:07:08.486
I'd just like to make a couple of comments

00:07:08.486 --> 00:07:10.800
on personnel issues,

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:12.060
starting with the non-executives.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:13.950
I'm just delighted to announce actually

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:17.163
that Mike Coupe and Jeremy Townsend,

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:20.580
their terms have been extended now

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:22.560
for a second term of office.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:24.510
This will allow Jeremy
to continue chairing

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:25.500
the Audit and Risk committee,

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:28.533
providing very much
valued business continuity

00:07:28.533 --> 00:07:30.600
as we manage this merger

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.540
and organisational restructure.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:36.300
Mike will continue to provide
his very valuable insights

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:37.890
and commercial experience
and particularly to

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.110
the Data Digital and Technology committee

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:41.850
and the new NHS England committee,

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:43.590
and also the Quality Committee.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:45.120
So thank you to to both of you

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.570
for agreeing to serve another term.

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:50.850
I'm also delighted that
we received clearance

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:53.458
for David Behan to continue
until the end of August

00:07:53.458 --> 00:07:54.810
next year.

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:59.640
And I know he is keen to
continue to support Navina

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:03.810
on embedding health education
into the new organisation.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:06.540
And for me, even more
importantly actually,

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:08.910
on the next stages of
execution and implementation

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:10.590
of the long-term workforce plan,

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:15.390
which is an essential
delivery for the NHS.

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:18.660
And then for new non-executives
coming to the board,

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.000
just to say that we are now

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.430
in the final stages of clearance.

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:25.860
So we have interviewed, we've shortlisted,

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:30.860
we've made submissions of those
candidates who we proposed

00:08:30.883 --> 00:08:34.650
have the potential to
come onto this board.

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:37.020
Those approvals are now between Number 10

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:40.500
and the cabinet office and
the Department of Health.

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:42.060
And I would hope actually
that we'll be able to,

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:44.280
I'll be able to update the board very soon

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:46.267
on which of those candidates actually

00:08:46.267 --> 00:08:48.303
are going to be appointed.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:51.959
In executive personnel, can I just,

00:08:51.959 --> 00:08:53.190
where's Vin?

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:56.550
I'd like to welcome Vin
Diwakar to his first board

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:58.890
as Interim Chief Transformation Officer.

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:02.070
Vin had had been working
very closely with Tim Ferris

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:04.710
in his role as medical
director for transformation

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:07.440
and medical director for secondary care.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.200
And Vin continues to provide
very strong leadership

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:12.270
on our transformation priorities,

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:14.940
including strengthening
the effective use of data

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.340
to deliver improved care through projects

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:20.130
such as the Federated Data Platform.

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:21.810
And Vin, my thanks to you,

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:23.210
for all you are doing there.

00:09:24.067 --> 00:09:27.180
I'm also very grateful that
Jim Mackey is also joining us

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:31.680
as as Interim COO while we
appoint the role permanently.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:34.680
And Jim will be supported by
Sarah-Jane Marsh in her role

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:36.720
as Deputy Chief Operating Officer,

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:39.060
and they'll be up updating
us on elective recovery

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.850
and winter preparedness
later on in the agenda.

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:43.920
I think Sarah-Jane is also still

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:45.960
the National Director of
Urgent Emergency Care.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:49.500
So both of you extremely busy executives

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:50.673
and our thanks to you.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:54.000
Just some highlights since
the last board meeting,

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.580
we've had two primary care

00:09:56.580 --> 00:10:00.450
or stakeholder breakfasts round tables,

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:01.860
and they predominantly focused

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:05.607
on discussing changes
to older people's care

00:10:05.607 --> 00:10:08.070
and we were joined by
community nursing colleagues,

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:09.510
experts in geriatric care,

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:12.840
from both the NHS and the
voluntary and community sectors,

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:14.460
by allied health professionals,

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:16.350
and representatives for care homes,

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:19.260
and local councils in
primary and community care,

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:22.050
and also a number of patient
representative groups.

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:24.480
These were very, very good sessions.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:26.010
Some of the key takeaways,

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:29.010
the importance of early
intervention and prevention,

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:33.793
and also the vital role that
community assets play in this.

00:10:33.793 --> 00:10:36.930
A workforce to think about
skills rather than roles,

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:40.740
that was a very important reflection.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:44.070
Importance of flexibility of
commissioning for local areas.

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:45.870
So what's the point of intervention,

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:48.720
what's the place at
which this best offers.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:49.830
And then healthy ageing,

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:52.560
how should the NHS work
better with its partners

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:55.263
to make the most of every single contact.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:10:59.190
The two strategy sessions
that the board held

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:01.020
on the 5th of September and yesterday,

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:02.123
actually the 4th of October,

00:11:02.123 --> 00:11:06.510
we were looking at specific
areas for transformation.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:09.062
So looking at demand management

00:11:09.062 --> 00:11:12.600
through a focus on cohorts of population

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:14.580
rather than just pathways

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:17.040
and where we can improve
our offer to patients

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:20.940
while also creating a more
sustainable NHS for the future.

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:23.790
And the first session looked
at older people's care,

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:25.140
it comes up again,

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:26.877
and also primary care transformation,

00:11:26.877 --> 00:11:29.730
and the board discussed
what key enabling factors

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:32.910
might be needed to make
a significant step change

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:34.770
in both of these areas.

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:36.000
And in particular,

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.100
what is it that the NHS needs to do

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:42.000
to support local
innovation at place level.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.910
The second session looked
at shifting acute care

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:46.350
out of hospital

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:50.280
and planned care through the
framework of supporting, again,

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:53.103
older people with more complex needs.

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:55.770
I'll speed up a bit now.

00:11:55.770 --> 00:11:58.500
So I apologise if I've
been going on a little.

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:00.810
I was delighted to be invited
by Professor Nick Maynard

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:02.250
to give a keynote speak

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:05.490
at the AUGIS Annual
Scientific Meeting in Oxford.

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:07.830
And then I visited the John
Radcliffe Hospital site

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:12.690
where I was shown the
ambulatory assessment unit.

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:15.480
I also visited cellular
pathology and histology

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:16.557
with Annie Sykes and her colleagues.

00:12:16.557 --> 00:12:19.980
And I had a very good meeting
with the chair and CEO of BOB,

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:24.360
or Buckinghamshire,
Oxfordshire and Berkshire ICS.

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:25.560
Separately, and with Amanda,

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:28.732
I joined the NHS Assembly
first session reflecting on

00:12:28.732 --> 00:12:31.263
the NHS at 75 and it's really
important in that assembly

00:12:31.263 --> 00:12:34.890
to hear the voices of
patient representatives

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:38.400
and experts about their experience.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:40.740
Separately, I've had a Q&amp;A session

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:45.740
with the Confed Network of
Mental Health trust chairs.

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:48.810
I shared the board's priorities

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:51.120
with the Mental Health trust chairs

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:53.580
and stressed the aim of the restructure,

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:54.420
as I talked about earlier,

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:58.020
was to reduce bureaucracy
and to enable and reduce

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:00.510
that sort of circular
reporting that goes on

00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:03.240
and improve connectivity
to the front line.

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:04.470
And they highlighted back to me

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:05.760
their main issues of concern

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:07.350
and many of those are similar to those

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:09.000
we see across the service.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:10.800
So questions of workforce,

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.930
very clear financial pressures,

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:16.083
focusing on staff wellbeing
to improve retention,

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:19.800
and generally actually
surging levels of demand

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:21.000
in mental health, which we know,

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.603
is a very significant
challenge that the NHS faces.

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:29.970
I had a very good meeting
with the crossbench peers,

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:33.870
that my thanks to Mary for enabling that,

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:35.577
and that was a very good
exchange of views actually.

00:13:35.577 --> 00:13:37.530
And a number of very
good questions were asked

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:39.420
by members of the House of Lords,

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:40.253
which we're taking away.

00:13:40.253 --> 00:13:43.170
We're following up on
some of those questions.

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:44.880
And earlier this week I did meet

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:48.627
with the new Chair of NHS
Professionals, Dr. Helen Phillips,

00:13:48.627 --> 00:13:51.060
and the Chief Executive, Nicola McQueen.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:53.321
And I met them with Navina

00:13:53.321 --> 00:13:56.730
and we spoke about the future
strategy of NHS Professionals

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:59.190
and how we can improve our relationship

00:13:59.190 --> 00:14:00.323
and make better use of their service

00:14:00.323 --> 00:14:02.010
to help with workforce issues.

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:04.470
So the better management of bank

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:06.120
and actually improve management

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:08.103
of international recruitment.

00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:13.260
So let me finish with
those chairs comments.

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:15.960
The agenda today I think is a good agenda.

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:17.670
Again, operational performance update.

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:20.190
Then a financial performance update.

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:21.120
We're going to have a session

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:23.280
on learning disability
in the autism programme

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:26.220
and I welcome Tom Cahill on that.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:27.930
We're going to look at the
three year delivery plan

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:30.720
for maternity and neonatal services,

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:31.890
which Ruth will lead on,

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:35.190
but she's joined by Kate
Brintworth and Duncan Burton

00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:37.080
and Ngozi Edi-Osagie.

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:40.080
And then the delivery
plan for recovering urgent

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:41.040
and emergency care.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:42.570
So a very good agenda.

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:44.520
We also, this morning's meeting,

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:48.510
we did touch on a series of
other items in private board.

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:51.960
Winter plan update, the
Q1 business plan report,

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:54.000
execution of the long-term workforce plan,

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:57.690
the implementation plan led
by Navina with David's support

00:14:57.690 --> 00:14:59.640
and operational planning guidance,

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:02.910
as well as a discussion
on our current progress

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:04.980
with the Federated Data Platform.

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:07.830
So at that point, thank
you for humouring me.

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:11.283
I'm going to hand to the Chief
Executive for her report.

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:14.160
- Great, thank you very
much indeed, Richard.

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:17.743
And just to echo your welcome
to both Vin and to Jim

00:15:18.592 --> 00:15:20.490
in their first board interim roles.

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:22.590
It's great to have them both alongside us.

00:15:24.277 --> 00:15:27.660
There's a few areas, Richard,
I'd like to pick up if I may,

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:32.580
starting with what has happened
at Countess of Chester.

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:35.460
So Richard has already talked
about some of the issues

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:37.920
that we've been looking
at since the verdict

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:39.870
in the Lucy Letby case.

00:15:39.870 --> 00:15:42.300
And I know this isn't
lost on anybody here,

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:43.650
but of course at the heart of this,

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:47.040
are families who have
experienced unimaginable pain

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:49.800
and will continue to do so
or likely continue to do so

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:52.260
for many years in the future.

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:55.650
So we are, I think all
absolutely committed

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:57.090
to doing everything that we can

00:15:57.090 --> 00:16:00.000
to prevent anything like
this happening again.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.190
Clearly as a board, as an executive group,

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:04.800
indeed as a wider NHS
leadership community,

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:08.250
we have spent a lot of time
over the last few weeks

00:16:08.250 --> 00:16:11.520
thinking about, even at this early stage,

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:14.340
what we can learn, what we should do,

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:16.440
and that's obviously in advance

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:19.350
of the Thirlwall inquiry starting.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:21.960
That's also true of the experience

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:23.940
of Martha Mills and her family,

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:27.330
which has come to prominence
in the last few weeks

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:30.390
and has highlighted, again,
amongst other things,

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:34.350
just that need to make sure
that the voices of patients

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:36.360
and their families are
properly listened to.

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:38.170
Which I know is a theme
we're going to come back to

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:40.080
when we'll talk about maternity later.

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:42.950
So I would like to just
thank, if I may, Ruth,

00:16:42.950 --> 00:16:46.860
Steve Powis and also Aiden
Fowler for their leadership

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:49.590
on these issues over the
past couple of months

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:50.423
in particular.

00:16:50.423 --> 00:16:51.690
But also the scores of people

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:54.240
who've been lending their
expertise and experience

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:56.880
to the discussions that we've been having.

00:16:56.880 --> 00:16:59.040
The themes that have come through,

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:01.260
I think consistently from the discussions,

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:05.490
have centred around culture and leadership

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:07.080
as well as the obvious things

00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:09.750
around things like governance and data,

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:12.390
but also how we can make sure

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:15.870
that in focusing on
culture and leadership,

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:19.140
we are unlocking that much
sort of broader set of issues

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:21.630
that then flow from that
around patient safety,

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:24.570
quality and experience more generally.

00:17:24.570 --> 00:17:26.490
So colleagues on the
board I know will know

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:30.610
that I have convened two meetings

00:17:32.826 --> 00:17:35.580
with a fantastic group of colleagues

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:37.140
from not just within the NHS

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:40.380
but our wider partners and experts

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:43.887
to specifically talk about
regulation leadership

00:17:43.887 --> 00:17:44.730
and management,

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:47.910
but also to talk about patient safety.

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:50.940
What we heard, what I heard
in that meeting and elsewhere,

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:54.000
is that there is I think a broad consensus

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:57.300
that alongside accountability for leaders,

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:59.280
which may include regulation

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:02.250
there also needs to be
support to meet them.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:05.370
And that includes ongoing
training and development.

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:07.350
So we are going to continue
to work with partners

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:11.340
to develop this work both
on leadership management,

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:12.800
and that's obviously very
much part of the work

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:15.300
that Navina has been
leading on implementing

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:18.600
the recommendations from the
Messenger Review last year,

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:20.100
but also we're going to continue to work

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:22.080
with Steve, Ruth, Aiden,

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:24.591
on what we can do now to reinforce

00:18:24.591 --> 00:18:26.670
the importance of patient safety

00:18:26.670 --> 00:18:29.160
and to make sure that alongside, as I say,

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:32.280
the ongoing work on things
like data and governance,

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:34.197
we are really putting
the listening to patients

00:18:34.197 --> 00:18:37.503
and their families at the
heart of everything that we do.

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:40.410
Richard, you talked
about industrial action,

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:42.480
but I would like to say, if I may,

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.700
just a little more about it.

00:18:44.700 --> 00:18:46.500
Of course that has been, I think,

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:47.880
operationally for the NHS,

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:52.590
very much the top focus for
the last couple of weeks,

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:54.840
but it's required significant focus

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:57.720
over the last now 10 months.

00:18:57.720 --> 00:19:01.290
Local teams have done an extraordinary job

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:04.260
in managing the disruption
and supporting colleagues.

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:08.070
But we do know that with
every period of action

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:09.540
things get harder.

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:13.830
So more than 60 days have been
directly impacted by strikes

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:15.510
involving multiple organisations,

00:19:15.510 --> 00:19:17.940
with each period obviously
causing disruption,

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:20.670
not just during action
but in the run up to

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:24.960
and the aftermath of those days.

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:27.240
And since the beginning of strikes,

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:31.650
more than half of the weeks
since industrial action began

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:34.350
have had some form of strike action.

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:36.150
And some of those have been significant,

00:19:36.150 --> 00:19:38.070
some of them have been
a bit more localised,

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:40.293
but they have all had an impact.

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:42.300
Worth noting, I think,

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:44.580
that since the beginning of July,

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:48.300
eight out of 14 weeks have
been directly affected

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:51.330
and we haven't gone
even four weeks straight

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:53.100
without a strike.

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:56.190
So we know that more than
a million appointments

00:19:56.190 --> 00:19:57.270
have been rescheduled,

00:19:57.270 --> 00:20:00.210
but we also know that
significantly more appointments

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:02.430
and procedures have been displaced,

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:07.050
potentially impacting hundreds
of thousands more patients.

00:20:07.050 --> 00:20:09.690
We've also seen though
that through the, again,

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:12.210
incredible hard work of NHS staff,

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:15.466
the overall published
waiting list was flat

00:20:15.466 --> 00:20:16.787
towards the end of last year.

00:20:16.787 --> 00:20:19.170
I know Jim will say more about this,

00:20:19.170 --> 00:20:24.150
but in fact once you adjust for
usual seasonal fluctuations,

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:29.150
the list did stabilise between
January and May of this year.

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:33.000
But clearly that has not been able to be,

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:34.320
could not be sustained,

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:38.160
given the sheer scale of strike impact.

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:40.770
The NHS has now seen hospital doctors

00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:45.540
undertake 720 hours of
industrial action since March.

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:50.400
That is equivalent to facing
a full calendar month 24/7

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:54.180
without a significant proportion
of our medical workforce.

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:59.180
And of course this is having
an impact beyond planned care.

00:20:59.490 --> 00:21:02.220
So in emergency care
we know that the focus

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:03.840
has been very much on patient safety,

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:05.700
seeing patients as quickly as possible

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:07.920
through the front door on strike days.

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:11.880
But that has stretched staffing
very thin in other areas,

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:14.490
which we know has had direct impact,

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:17.310
not least on slowing down
discharge in some areas.

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:19.530
And it's also meant
clinicians have had less time

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:21.720
to focus on their usual work.

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:25.080
So as it has been throughout our priority

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:27.600
and we know the focus,

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:30.180
I know colleagues know the
focus of our discussions

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:31.980
with both unions and government

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:34.980
continues to be patient safety.

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:37.170
Both for those who need
emergency treatment

00:21:37.170 --> 00:21:39.270
and those waiting for planned care.

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:41.880
And we all want to see, I think,

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:45.120
serious discussions resume
between unions and government

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:46.620
and with winter approaching,

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:49.203
that cannot happen soon enough.

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:52.980
So it's important, Richard, though,

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:56.400
that as you have been in being open

00:21:56.400 --> 00:21:58.560
about the impact that strikes
are having on recovery,

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:00.150
particularly elective care,

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:02.370
but not only elective care,

00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:06.030
we also just talk about
the cost of that action.

00:22:06.030 --> 00:22:08.640
So we have had some
recognition from government

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:10.740
of the additional costs.

00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:13.380
We aren't taking our foot off the gas

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:17.910
despite the obviousness of
the pressure on people's time,

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:22.440
but also the obviousness
and increasingly clear

00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:24.450
financial stretch that it is bringing.

00:22:24.450 --> 00:22:26.400
So I think it is worth us highlighting,

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:30.540
as we later talk about some
of the financial impacts,

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:32.310
but it is worth us highlighting

00:22:32.310 --> 00:22:35.490
that actually despite
all of those pressures,

00:22:35.490 --> 00:22:37.620
for example in diagnostics,

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:41.670
the NHS has delivered 25 million key scans

00:22:41.670 --> 00:22:44.040
over the 12 months to July.

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:47.190
That is 50% more than a decade ago,

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:49.050
and waits of more than 65 weeks,

00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:50.460
which is the thing that
we've been focusing

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:52.310
on particularly in elective recovery,

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:55.950
by the time we got to the end of July,

00:22:55.950 --> 00:23:00.950
it's actually 59% down on
the peak from June, 2021.

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:06.500
So loads more work to do in
an increasingly difficult

00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:09.120
environment on elective care.

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:12.000
But actually worth saying that
the NHS has done, I think,

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:14.670
an extraordinary job
in maintaining activity

00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:16.230
despite that.

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:18.360
Despite a very pressurised summer,

00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:21.510
we are seeing faster
ambulance response times

00:23:21.510 --> 00:23:22.950
and improvement, although small,

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:25.410
in four hour performance
compared with last year

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:26.820
and in primary care too,

00:23:26.820 --> 00:23:27.930
we've been averaging over

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:30.270
a million more appointments a month

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:32.010
and more than half continue to be

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:34.230
the same or the next day.

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:36.750
So we need to talk about the implication

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:41.750
of where we are with the
impact on performance.

00:23:43.350 --> 00:23:44.910
Jim will pick that up in a moment

00:23:44.910 --> 00:23:47.340
and we certainly need to
just acknowledge the pressure

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:48.840
it's putting on the financials.

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:51.420
But I think I did want
to take this opportunity

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:53.580
to give credit to
colleagues across the NHS

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:55.470
who have just worked so unbelievably hard

00:23:55.470 --> 00:23:58.800
to do the right thing
for patients throughout.

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:01.170
So in maintaining momentum, Richard,

00:24:01.170 --> 00:24:02.340
on the urgent emergency care recovery plan

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:04.950
we had a very good conversation about that

00:24:04.950 --> 00:24:06.390
in our meeting earlier,

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:09.870
which includes delivering on
the additional extra beds,

00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:10.703
for example,

00:24:10.703 --> 00:24:14.100
will be particularly important
as we get closer to winter.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:15.891
But also worth highlighting

00:24:15.891 --> 00:24:18.480
that another thing that is a critical part

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:20.250
of our urgent emergency care recovery plan

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:22.170
is helping people to stay well.

00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:26.610
So our winter vaccination
campaign is hugely important.

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:28.980
Last winter we were under real pressure,

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:30.300
as colleagues will recall,

00:24:30.300 --> 00:24:33.020
in January from both flu and Covid.

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:35.130
So we had the twindemic last year,

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:37.680
and that's why we had planned
the campaign this year

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:41.010
to better align peak
protection with that period.

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:43.650
That obviously had to
change due to the emergence

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:45.090
of a new COVID variant.

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:49.200
And Steve and his team have
done a really excellent job

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:51.510
in pulling forward the campaign,

00:24:51.510 --> 00:24:54.840
already now delivering
3.6 million Covid boosters

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:58.020
and 5.7 million flu vaccines.

00:24:58.020 --> 00:24:59.940
As usual, frontline healthcare,

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:02.880
health and care workers are
eligible for both vaccines

00:25:02.880 --> 00:25:04.740
and I know again, Ruth, Steve,

00:25:04.740 --> 00:25:06.510
and our other chief professional officers

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:09.750
will once again be lending
their voices to local campaigns

00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:12.090
to achieve high levels of uptake

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:14.100
so that we are looking after our staff

00:25:14.100 --> 00:25:16.893
as well as we possibly can
as we head into winter.

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:19.170
Also worth saying,

00:25:19.170 --> 00:25:21.300
I've only got three more things I promise,

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:23.760
but worth saying a bit about our plans

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:27.180
for a Federated Data Platform for the NHS,

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:30.000
which Vin is now leading in his new role

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.850
and may want to say a bit more on,

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:37.170
but we've run an incredibly
thorough procurement process

00:25:37.170 --> 00:25:38.760
over the past few months

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:42.510
and we are expecting that to
conclude in the coming weeks.

00:25:42.510 --> 00:25:46.500
I think there is a broad
consensus on the need for the NHS

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:48.690
to better link up the data we have

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:51.390
to deliver care more effectively.

00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:54.420
But we do know that we
need to take people with us

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:55.950
on that journey.

00:25:55.950 --> 00:25:58.860
So that's why last week we also announced

00:25:58.860 --> 00:26:01.680
a major engagement programme
starting in the new year

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:05.340
to gather public views on
digital and data transformation

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:06.693
in the NHS.

00:26:07.770 --> 00:26:10.650
Final two things, we should mark,

00:26:10.650 --> 00:26:13.020
and I'm sure Steve will want
to say a bit more about this,

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:16.110
but we should just mark
also the welcome news

00:26:16.110 --> 00:26:17.040
from government this week

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:18.930
that they'll be taking action to reduce

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:21.750
the number of young people
who take up smoking.

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:24.060
This was a recommendation of a review.

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:26.310
I know colleagues will
remember this last year.

00:26:26.310 --> 00:26:30.930
And lots of colleagues in the
NHS nationally and locally

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:32.550
fed into that review.

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:35.400
So I'm confident that it will be welcomed

00:26:35.400 --> 00:26:37.770
across the service.

00:26:37.770 --> 00:26:40.740
The human and financial costs of smoking

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:44.130
are seen in every part
of the health service

00:26:44.130 --> 00:26:47.400
and play a significant part
in health inequalities.

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:49.560
Something we've been addressing

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:53.490
with our lung scanning trucks
in supermarket car parks

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:56.220
and elsewhere offering
current and ex-smokers

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:59.520
the opportunity to have
an on the spot check.

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:01.950
So although none of this,

00:27:01.950 --> 00:27:05.160
and none of that excellent
and really important work

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:08.190
is going to become unnecessary overnight,

00:27:08.190 --> 00:27:09.723
or indeed become,

00:27:11.341 --> 00:27:15.180
this isn't an overnight fix.

00:27:15.180 --> 00:27:18.930
Nonetheless this is a really significant

00:27:18.930 --> 00:27:21.630
long-term intervention and I, again,

00:27:21.630 --> 00:27:24.300
I'm sure Steve will want to
say a bit more about that.

00:27:24.300 --> 00:27:25.320
Finally, Richard,

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:27.210
you've mentioned the work that's going on

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:30.150
within our own organisation
to create the new NHS England,

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:33.090
bringing together five
previous organisations.

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:34.890
I know I've said this before, in fact,

00:27:34.890 --> 00:27:37.590
I think I say this every
time we get together,

00:27:37.590 --> 00:27:40.860
but I do want to thank my
colleagues within NHS England

00:27:40.860 --> 00:27:44.160
because this is a period
of significant uncertainty.

00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:48.510
It has been a huge change, as you've said,

00:27:48.510 --> 00:27:51.180
and at the moment we do know
that an awful lot of people

00:27:51.180 --> 00:27:53.880
are uncertain about their future roles,

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:55.830
supporting others who are
going through the process,

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:59.040
or are dealing with increased
workloads and capacity gaps,

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:02.190
yet continuing to come to work every day

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:04.740
to focus on the difference
that they can make

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:08.640
to make the NHS work as
well as it possibly can

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:11.070
and to support others on the front line.

00:28:11.070 --> 00:28:14.190
So my thanks to them
and my thanks to Steve

00:28:14.190 --> 00:28:15.660
for leading the process,

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:17.640
and also now for launching what feels like

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:20.430
a really timely and a really
important piece of work

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:23.370
across the organisation on
how we create a new culture,

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:25.710
new ways of working for
the new organisation

00:28:25.710 --> 00:28:28.530
that are going to enable us
to deliver on our ambitions

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:30.810
for the new NHS England.

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:33.000
- So, Amanda, thank you.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:36.060
I will ask Vin if you want to
comment on FDP in a moment.

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:37.920
And Steve on smoking.

00:28:37.920 --> 00:28:40.860
Just on that last point,
just to reemphasize it,

00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:42.540
I think it's often too easily said,

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:47.490
but little understood just
how immense a programme

00:28:47.490 --> 00:28:49.730
of reorganisation transformation it is

00:28:50.614 --> 00:28:52.770
to essentially merge five,

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:55.530
it's five entities
actually coming together

00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:58.170
to conform policies, to
bring systems together,

00:28:58.170 --> 00:29:00.300
make choices to conform data,

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:02.580
to consolidate the relevant programmes,

00:29:02.580 --> 00:29:04.530
and the interaction from the centre,

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:08.280
a smaller still expert
centre with the systems.

00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:09.690
And to do that at the same time

00:29:09.690 --> 00:29:12.462
as reducing headcount by 35%.

00:29:12.462 --> 00:29:13.410
I mean, 35%.

00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:16.260
It'd be hard to think of many other

00:29:16.260 --> 00:29:19.290
equivalent transformation undertakings

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:22.140
that have been pursued or achieved,

00:29:22.140 --> 00:29:23.826
certainly in the public sector,

00:29:23.826 --> 00:29:25.950
or in the private sector actually.

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:28.917
And yet the machine continues to run.

00:29:28.917 --> 00:29:30.858
So, again, my tribute to the-

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:32.813
You should.

00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:37.600
- Just to actually make the point that

00:29:38.460 --> 00:29:40.410
this was the plan that was put forward

00:29:40.410 --> 00:29:43.110
by the chief executive
and led by the executives.

00:29:43.110 --> 00:29:43.950
It wasn't imposed,

00:29:43.950 --> 00:29:45.330
it's what they sought to do,

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:47.490
and I think we would never got anywhere

00:29:47.490 --> 00:29:48.930
unless that actually happened.

00:29:48.930 --> 00:29:51.333
So I think we forget
that. It was your plan.

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:55.350
And that's important that we
acknowledge and recognise that.

00:29:55.350 --> 00:29:56.760
- Well said, Wol. Thank you.

00:29:56.760 --> 00:29:58.950
So Vin, having given you warning,

00:29:58.950 --> 00:30:02.015
and it's your first outing
in this board meeting,

00:30:02.015 --> 00:30:04.170
just any comments you'd like to add

00:30:04.170 --> 00:30:05.820
on the Federated Data Platform?

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:07.020
- Yeah, thank you chair.

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:09.150
So I think the first thing to say

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:12.480
is just to remind everyone
that I think everyone agrees,

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:16.448
the NHS has fragmented
systems for managing data

00:30:16.448 --> 00:30:19.590
and that causes immense
frustration to clinicians

00:30:19.590 --> 00:30:22.620
and it causes immense
frustration to patients.

00:30:22.620 --> 00:30:25.260
And it's really important
that we don't make

00:30:25.260 --> 00:30:28.110
the Federated Data Platform
something that it isn't.

00:30:28.110 --> 00:30:31.800
It is software and it is
software that enables you

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:34.260
to view data from different sources

00:30:34.260 --> 00:30:37.350
in a single view on your computer screen.

00:30:37.350 --> 00:30:40.710
It makes it easier for you
to manage your patients,

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:42.810
whether that's bringing
down a waiting list

00:30:42.810 --> 00:30:46.380
or making it easier to discharge
patients from a hospital

00:30:46.380 --> 00:30:48.510
so that all of your caregivers have access

00:30:48.510 --> 00:30:52.020
to the same information, no data moves.

00:30:52.020 --> 00:30:56.760
It is software that enables
you to see data in one place.

00:30:56.760 --> 00:30:59.020
The second thing I think
to remember is that

00:31:00.300 --> 00:31:04.620
it has been procured by
the NHS, for the NHS.

00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:07.950
The procurement was run
over several months,

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:11.310
over 30 different people scored the bids

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:14.070
that came in from potential suppliers.

00:31:14.070 --> 00:31:17.400
No single person was able
to influence the decision.

00:31:17.400 --> 00:31:20.310
Independent reviewers
reviewed individual sections

00:31:20.310 --> 00:31:23.640
and then those scores
were then aggregated.

00:31:23.640 --> 00:31:26.400
I think the public can have
confidence in the software

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:29.850
because at the same time as
we are doing this procurement,

00:31:29.850 --> 00:31:33.180
we are procuring privacy
enhancing software

00:31:33.180 --> 00:31:35.550
that we have therefore two different ways

00:31:35.550 --> 00:31:37.080
of protecting your data

00:31:37.080 --> 00:31:40.080
and access to the data is
very strictly controlled

00:31:40.080 --> 00:31:43.170
so that only those who
need the data can see it.

00:31:43.170 --> 00:31:45.693
And in particular it's only your clinician

00:31:45.693 --> 00:31:47.970
who can view personal level data.

00:31:47.970 --> 00:31:50.430
It cannot be reviewed by anyone else.

00:31:50.430 --> 00:31:53.670
And it is software,

00:31:53.670 --> 00:31:55.320
and what we've actually procured

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:58.410
are licences for every trust in NHS

00:31:58.410 --> 00:32:00.780
in every integrated care system.

00:32:00.780 --> 00:32:03.210
But the control of the data remains

00:32:03.210 --> 00:32:06.000
with the organisations
who control the data now.

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:09.360
So nothing has changed in
terms of data security.

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:10.680
And the final thing I just might say

00:32:10.680 --> 00:32:13.080
is I think the public
can have confidence in it

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:14.100
for two reasons.

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:15.780
One is, as Amanda says,

00:32:15.780 --> 00:32:18.150
that we have announced 2 million pounds

00:32:18.150 --> 00:32:20.458
to conduct a public dialogue

00:32:20.458 --> 00:32:23.700
that is part of the Data
Saves Lives campaign.

00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:28.290
And secondly that we will be
setting up an advisory group

00:32:28.290 --> 00:32:30.720
composed not just with
members of the public

00:32:30.720 --> 00:32:32.994
but those who are experts in data

00:32:32.994 --> 00:32:36.810
from various organisations to
advise us on any future uses

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:39.870
to make sure that those are
properly debated and controlled.

00:32:39.870 --> 00:32:40.703
But I think,

00:32:40.703 --> 00:32:42.810
and I think many around
this table will agree,

00:32:42.810 --> 00:32:45.810
this will have a transformational
impact on the NHS.

00:32:45.810 --> 00:32:46.800
As a clinician myself,

00:32:46.800 --> 00:32:48.480
I don't know a single clinician

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:49.890
who has not used the software

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:52.200
and said it has completely
changed their lives

00:32:52.200 --> 00:32:53.800
in the way they manage patients.

00:32:54.660 --> 00:32:56.013
- Vin, thank you very much.

00:32:57.540 --> 00:33:01.470
Steve, do you want to comment
on the smoking policy?

00:33:01.470 --> 00:33:04.020
- Like any clinician,

00:33:04.020 --> 00:33:07.590
I have spent my clinical
career witnessing firsthand

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:12.590
the terrible impact that
smoking has on people's lives.

00:33:12.840 --> 00:33:15.690
I think everybody knows it's
the major cause of lung cancer

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:19.260
but it also increases the
risk of many other cancers,

00:33:19.260 --> 00:33:21.960
heart attacks, strokes, dementia,

00:33:21.960 --> 00:33:25.230
stillbirth during
pregnancy, the list goes on.

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:27.240
And so that's why, personally,

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:29.220
and I'm sure everybody in this room,

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:34.110
will welcome the decision announced

00:33:34.110 --> 00:33:37.200
by the prime minister yesterday
to bring forward legislation

00:33:37.200 --> 00:33:41.370
to progressively raise
the legal age of smoking.

00:33:41.370 --> 00:33:43.680
And I think that that will be seen

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:46.983
as a truly historic, momentous
public health moment.

00:33:48.090 --> 00:33:51.630
I am absolutely confident
that it will be welcomed

00:33:51.630 --> 00:33:55.500
by clinicians and clinical
leaders across the board

00:33:55.500 --> 00:33:58.110
and we will certainly be
doing everything that we can

00:33:58.110 --> 00:34:03.033
to ensure that it gets through
parliament and is enacted.

00:34:05.310 --> 00:34:08.380
It also focuses once again on
the importance of prevention

00:34:09.953 --> 00:34:12.810
and of course there are
some things that the NHS

00:34:12.810 --> 00:34:15.000
can't do in terms of prevention.

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:16.620
Those population health measures,

00:34:16.620 --> 00:34:18.090
such as the one announced yesterday,

00:34:18.090 --> 00:34:20.610
we can influence but
that's not in our control.

00:34:20.610 --> 00:34:23.070
But there are many things
that are in our control.

00:34:23.070 --> 00:34:24.750
So for instance, for tobacco,

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:28.140
we have been rolling
out tobacco cessation,

00:34:28.140 --> 00:34:31.650
smoking cessation services across the NHS,

00:34:31.650 --> 00:34:34.350
and we will be continuing to do that.

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:36.210
And of course not in the speech yesterday,

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:38.703
but in the material in the announcement,

00:34:39.659 --> 00:34:41.640
the details of the
announcement that went with it,

00:34:41.640 --> 00:34:45.000
was an almost doubling of the funding

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:47.730
to local government for
smoking cessation services.

00:34:47.730 --> 00:34:48.960
And that's really important for us

00:34:48.960 --> 00:34:51.960
because our smoking
cessation services in the NHS

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:52.809
work hand in hand

00:34:52.809 --> 00:34:56.040
with those local government
cessation services.

00:34:56.040 --> 00:34:58.530
So that is to be welcomed, too.

00:34:58.530 --> 00:35:01.080
And we will continue, as you would expect,

00:35:01.080 --> 00:35:04.260
to focus particularly on the
secondary prevention elements

00:35:04.260 --> 00:35:06.608
that we can focus on in the NHS

00:35:06.608 --> 00:35:10.290
where we contact patients
on a one-to-one basis

00:35:10.290 --> 00:35:12.660
rather than just those
population health measures.

00:35:12.660 --> 00:35:15.510
But clearly they are
both equally important.

00:35:15.510 --> 00:35:18.377
- Steve, thank you and
with no forewarning to you,

00:35:18.377 --> 00:35:21.150
I mean I spoke about industrial action

00:35:21.150 --> 00:35:23.200
and Amanda spoke about industrial action.

00:35:24.060 --> 00:35:25.683
We wrote to the BMA,

00:35:26.893 --> 00:35:30.030
and you and Ruth and Navina and Jim

00:35:30.030 --> 00:35:31.869
all signed the letter to the BMA.

00:35:31.869 --> 00:35:33.510
Are there any other
comments you'd like to add

00:35:33.510 --> 00:35:34.890
to what we've said on
the industrial action?

00:35:34.890 --> 00:35:36.000
- I think it's important,

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:39.630
Amanda has framed these
discussions around patient safety

00:35:39.630 --> 00:35:42.510
and one of our key roles as NHS England

00:35:42.510 --> 00:35:45.180
is to ensure that we support the NHS,

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:47.847
all organisations within the NHS,

00:35:47.847 --> 00:35:51.060
and all clinicians to
ensure that patient safety

00:35:51.060 --> 00:35:52.530
can be maintained.

00:35:52.530 --> 00:35:54.510
And I think it's our responsibility

00:35:54.510 --> 00:35:58.350
when we see risks to patient safety,

00:35:58.350 --> 00:36:01.526
either materialising or
at risk of materialising,

00:36:01.526 --> 00:36:02.703
to point that out.

00:36:03.660 --> 00:36:05.970
We do that regularly in
our updates to government,

00:36:05.970 --> 00:36:07.590
as Amanda has said,

00:36:07.590 --> 00:36:12.060
but we felt the time had come
to formally raise with the BMA

00:36:12.060 --> 00:36:15.000
a number of concerns that
are detailed in the letter

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:20.000
around how industrial
action is impacting upon NHS

00:36:20.487 --> 00:36:23.120
and particularly our
emergency and urgent services

00:36:23.120 --> 00:36:27.060
in patients who require
maybe not surgery tomorrow,

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:28.443
but in the next few weeks.

00:36:29.400 --> 00:36:32.760
We have been having ongoing
discussions with the BMA

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:36.450
operationally throughout strike actions.

00:36:36.450 --> 00:36:40.080
Those will continue and we will
be taking forward the issues

00:36:40.080 --> 00:36:42.480
that we raised as part
of those discussions.

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:43.430
- Steve, thank you.

00:36:44.370 --> 00:36:45.203
I won't get others to comment.

00:36:45.203 --> 00:36:47.190
I know, Ruth, you signed
the letter, as I said,

00:36:47.190 --> 00:36:48.630
and Jim, you signed it, and Navina.

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:51.930
It's very important as a
group of our four executives

00:36:51.930 --> 00:36:54.300
that your signatures to that,

00:36:54.300 --> 00:36:56.450
I think it was an
important step, actually.

00:36:57.660 --> 00:36:59.490
Can I move us on, though?

00:36:59.490 --> 00:37:01.980
I think I'm going to move to
performance update if we can.

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:05.700
So Jim, I think you are up
on operational performance,

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:06.533
I think.

00:37:06.533 --> 00:37:07.366
- Yeah, thanks Richard.

00:37:07.366 --> 00:37:09.630
So I'll continue the discussion really

00:37:09.630 --> 00:37:11.520
about what's been happening recently.

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:15.540
So the report covers July
for elective care and cancer,

00:37:15.540 --> 00:37:17.400
August for urgent care.

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:19.470
Both feel like years away.

00:37:19.470 --> 00:37:22.230
It's been a very, very busy summer,

00:37:22.230 --> 00:37:23.580
as you can see in the statistics,

00:37:23.580 --> 00:37:25.110
very busy in urgent care,

00:37:25.110 --> 00:37:28.560
especially in elective care and cancer.

00:37:28.560 --> 00:37:32.220
The NHS managed to maintain very, very,

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:33.960
surprisingly high levels of activity

00:37:33.960 --> 00:37:35.970
through all of the disruption.

00:37:35.970 --> 00:37:37.530
And you can see year on year,

00:37:37.530 --> 00:37:40.650
really impressive
reductions in long waits,

00:37:40.650 --> 00:37:43.677
stabilised a bit through
industrial action,

00:37:43.677 --> 00:37:46.290
and in urgent care improvements

00:37:46.290 --> 00:37:48.930
on category two response
teams and four hours.

00:37:48.930 --> 00:37:52.740
I'm sure Sarah-Jane will
mention them later on.

00:37:52.740 --> 00:37:56.790
We're also continuing to work
with the NHS all the time,

00:37:56.790 --> 00:37:59.547
just trying to navigate this complexity

00:37:59.547 --> 00:38:01.230
of industrial action,

00:38:01.230 --> 00:38:03.780
the churn associated
with industrial action,

00:38:03.780 --> 00:38:05.700
the stretch that comes with that.

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:08.170
And increasingly the
financial pressure that comes

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:09.177
with industrial action.

00:38:09.177 --> 00:38:11.820
And in recent weeks that's
been a very topical issue.

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:14.430
So we have spent a lot
of time with colleagues

00:38:14.430 --> 00:38:17.674
just helping with prioritisation focus,

00:38:17.674 --> 00:38:19.830
and as Steve has said,

00:38:19.830 --> 00:38:22.080
trying to make sure we're
all as safe as we can be

00:38:22.080 --> 00:38:23.130
in these times.

00:38:23.130 --> 00:38:27.270
Because the service since these
reports and during September

00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:29.130
has become very stretched

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:33.420
and I think everyone can see
that in their day-to-day work,

00:38:33.420 --> 00:38:36.960
but hugely impressive
performance in that context.

00:38:36.960 --> 00:38:38.430
If you'd said last year the NHS

00:38:38.430 --> 00:38:41.067
would have 11 consecutive
months of industrial action

00:38:41.067 --> 00:38:42.810
and still be on its feet,

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:45.063
I don't think anybody
would've actually believed it.

00:38:45.063 --> 00:38:47.370
It's really strong there.

00:38:47.370 --> 00:38:49.440
Awful lot to do, though,

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:51.960
in terms of delivering
the long term ambitions.

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:53.040
And on elective care,

00:38:53.040 --> 00:38:55.770
just a couple of additional factors.

00:38:55.770 --> 00:38:58.470
So one of the things we're
really still hanging onto

00:38:58.470 --> 00:38:59.520
and taking heart from,

00:38:59.520 --> 00:39:02.490
even when things like
list size have increased

00:39:02.490 --> 00:39:04.200
during industrial action

00:39:04.200 --> 00:39:06.150
and we've stabilised on long waits

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:10.380
is the 65 week cohort
falls very consistently,

00:39:10.380 --> 00:39:13.710
pretty much every week even
through industrial auction

00:39:13.710 --> 00:39:17.880
which is the next big goal
that we're aiming for.

00:39:17.880 --> 00:39:20.400
We're trying to do everything we can do

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.920
to get ahead of the game
for next year as well,

00:39:22.920 --> 00:39:26.485
where all the outpatient
reform really needs to kick in

00:39:26.485 --> 00:39:29.970
and we've got really strong
returns from the NHS there

00:39:29.970 --> 00:39:31.350
as requested in the summer.

00:39:31.350 --> 00:39:33.990
So great support there from colleagues

00:39:33.990 --> 00:39:36.660
and with Tim Brigg's
Further Faster programme,

00:39:36.660 --> 00:39:38.580
again, some evidence coming through.

00:39:38.580 --> 00:39:42.120
Real impact, although early days.

00:39:42.120 --> 00:39:44.100
And then just to top it off,

00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:46.740
we should remember as well
that this isn't a waiting list

00:39:46.740 --> 00:39:48.090
for surgery.

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:50.760
So it's often commented
upon as a waiting list

00:39:50.760 --> 00:39:52.170
of seven and a half million people

00:39:52.170 --> 00:39:54.090
waiting for elective surgery.

00:39:54.090 --> 00:39:56.250
That's not the case.

00:39:56.250 --> 00:39:59.430
Over 80% of the people
currently on the list

00:39:59.430 --> 00:40:01.230
will end their episode in outpatients

00:40:01.230 --> 00:40:03.150
or a diagnostic programme.

00:40:03.150 --> 00:40:04.920
That's exactly where we're focusing

00:40:04.920 --> 00:40:08.670
on trying to deliver change
for this next period.

00:40:08.670 --> 00:40:12.360
So we deliver long term change.

00:40:12.360 --> 00:40:14.130
And also the seven and a half million

00:40:14.130 --> 00:40:16.230
isn't seven and a half million people,

00:40:16.230 --> 00:40:18.720
it's just over six million people,

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:20.640
which we talk about regularly.

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:21.930
I think it is very important

00:40:21.930 --> 00:40:25.440
in terms of coordinating
their care appropriately

00:40:25.440 --> 00:40:27.180
but also not misleading the public

00:40:27.180 --> 00:40:31.140
who see that headline every month.

00:40:31.140 --> 00:40:33.660
Very finally, again to just give heart,

00:40:33.660 --> 00:40:35.010
I've had a couple of sessions this week

00:40:35.010 --> 00:40:36.270
with colleagues here,

00:40:36.270 --> 00:40:38.220
with colleagues in Sussex, for example,

00:40:38.220 --> 00:40:41.820
and also Kings who've been
challenged in different ways,

00:40:41.820 --> 00:40:44.070
financial challenges,
operational challenges,

00:40:44.070 --> 00:40:45.750
the challenge of industrial action,

00:40:45.750 --> 00:40:47.670
and both give real heart actually

00:40:47.670 --> 00:40:50.880
that people are really working very hard

00:40:50.880 --> 00:40:53.970
with complex decision-making,
prioritisation,

00:40:53.970 --> 00:40:55.620
really strong clinical engagement,

00:40:55.620 --> 00:40:57.660
and all of those conversations as well

00:40:57.660 --> 00:40:59.280
to make sure that we do the right thing

00:40:59.280 --> 00:41:01.893
in these complex times.

00:41:02.790 --> 00:41:03.623
- Very good.

00:41:03.623 --> 00:41:07.080
Can I open up for comments
or questions to Jim

00:41:07.080 --> 00:41:07.913
on operational performance?

00:41:07.913 --> 00:41:10.290
I mean I would open just with,

00:41:10.290 --> 00:41:12.500
it's referenced on paragraphs 15 and 16,

00:41:12.500 --> 00:41:15.060
of the report about diagnostic capacity.

00:41:15.060 --> 00:41:16.890
I realise one of them is a comment

00:41:16.890 --> 00:41:21.630
on the recent increasing
in our diagnostic capacity,

00:41:21.630 --> 00:41:25.500
but also the commitment to
get to 160 centres by March.

00:41:25.500 --> 00:41:28.200
I mean personally when
I came into this role,

00:41:28.200 --> 00:41:31.530
the fact that we have significantly below

00:41:31.530 --> 00:41:33.960
OECD average diagnostic equipment.

00:41:33.960 --> 00:41:35.970
So we're not even at average with OECD,

00:41:35.970 --> 00:41:36.803
we're significantly below that.

00:41:36.803 --> 00:41:39.420
I think about half the average of the OECD

00:41:39.420 --> 00:41:41.490
for diagnostic kit.

00:41:41.490 --> 00:41:42.690
It's really pleasing to see that.

00:41:42.690 --> 00:41:45.480
Your confident in that rollout?

00:41:45.480 --> 00:41:46.313
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:41:46.313 --> 00:41:48.414
In terms of the investment,

00:41:48.414 --> 00:41:50.610
that investment for diagnostics,

00:41:50.610 --> 00:41:51.960
but also in surgical hubs,

00:41:51.960 --> 00:41:54.240
a lot of them are just
coming on stream now.

00:41:54.240 --> 00:41:58.620
So, we've got those benefits
to key in eventually.

00:41:58.620 --> 00:42:00.600
Clearly it's a stretch on the workforce

00:42:00.600 --> 00:42:03.240
so we need to make sure
that it does present

00:42:03.240 --> 00:42:05.160
actual additionality overall

00:42:05.160 --> 00:42:07.170
and colleagues here are
working hard with a service

00:42:07.170 --> 00:42:08.520
to deliver that.

00:42:08.520 --> 00:42:09.750
There'll be a slight dilution

00:42:09.750 --> 00:42:11.460
because of industrial
action there as well.

00:42:11.460 --> 00:42:13.710
But yeah, very confident
that we'll deliver

00:42:13.710 --> 00:42:15.030
what we said we're going to do.

00:42:15.030 --> 00:42:15.900
- Thanks Jim.

00:42:15.900 --> 00:42:17.740
Wol I think you want to comment.

00:42:17.740 --> 00:42:19.170
Wol, then David.

00:42:19.170 --> 00:42:22.170
- Thank you. Thanks Jim.
Comprehensive report.

00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:25.230
This is going to be a hard
question, I apologise in advance,

00:42:25.230 --> 00:42:26.820
and it's your first meeting.

00:42:29.631 --> 00:42:31.873
So Amanda and Steve have given us

00:42:31.873 --> 00:42:35.378
a really interesting flavour
of the impact industrial action

00:42:35.378 --> 00:42:36.690
and there's a sense in your report

00:42:36.690 --> 00:42:39.000
that you are sort of
surprised and delighted

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:40.225
that despite all that

00:42:40.225 --> 00:42:42.420
it's been in many cases,

00:42:42.420 --> 00:42:45.750
remarkable it's gone forward
as well as it has done.

00:42:45.750 --> 00:42:47.704
But can you give us a flavour

00:42:47.704 --> 00:42:49.779
of some of the sort of the work

00:42:49.779 --> 00:42:51.420
that needs to be done pre and the post?

00:42:51.420 --> 00:42:54.363
Because in doing that, you
are giving up something.

00:42:55.410 --> 00:42:57.240
There's a trade off here somewhere.

00:42:57.240 --> 00:43:00.540
And you're storing up
problems for tomorrow

00:43:00.540 --> 00:43:01.410
and the day after and the day after that.

00:43:01.410 --> 00:43:03.780
So it's much more than a today problem

00:43:03.780 --> 00:43:05.130
then just getting through.

00:43:05.130 --> 00:43:07.020
Some of the stuff you're just not doing.

00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:07.853
- No, absolutely.

00:43:07.853 --> 00:43:09.707
So that's a great question, Wol,

00:43:09.707 --> 00:43:12.030
and you look at this a few different ways.

00:43:12.030 --> 00:43:14.940
One is in elective care you
need to be able to plan ahead.

00:43:14.940 --> 00:43:17.460
One of the really big
things that worked last year

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:22.069
was our commitment to try and
book patients in the cohort

00:43:22.069 --> 00:43:25.890
several weeks ahead and get
that certainty for everybody,

00:43:25.890 --> 00:43:27.840
for clinicians with a schedule

00:43:27.840 --> 00:43:29.606
and patients as well know when they're

00:43:29.606 --> 00:43:31.148
going to get treatment.

00:43:31.148 --> 00:43:34.110
Clearly when things are going
to get disrupted all the time,

00:43:34.110 --> 00:43:36.643
you're not quite sure when
industrial action's going

00:43:36.643 --> 00:43:38.160
to happen. The bill's in a hesitancy.

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:41.283
So first of all, you're less
productive than you want to be.

00:43:42.840 --> 00:43:46.230
If you talk to contact
staff, booking staff,

00:43:46.230 --> 00:43:48.090
medical secretaries,
those sorts of people,

00:43:48.090 --> 00:43:51.480
they are in a constant churn of cancelling

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:53.590
and rebooting all the time

00:43:54.450 --> 00:43:57.810
and having to tell disappointed
people, worried people,

00:43:57.810 --> 00:44:00.030
people who might be waiting
for a cancer diagnosis,

00:44:00.030 --> 00:44:00.863
et cetera,

00:44:00.863 --> 00:44:02.610
that things have being cancelled again.

00:44:02.610 --> 00:44:04.920
And we're going to try and get
them in as quickly as we can.

00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:09.210
But there's an uncertainty
all the time in all of that.

00:44:09.210 --> 00:44:11.370
We have been frustrated at times

00:44:11.370 --> 00:44:14.520
about the focus on just cancelled episodes

00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:17.070
because it really is a
very small proportion

00:44:17.070 --> 00:44:18.960
of the whole impact of this,

00:44:18.960 --> 00:44:21.720
because in the NHS,

00:44:21.720 --> 00:44:24.810
the service is just doing
this now all the time.

00:44:24.810 --> 00:44:28.320
It's churning in a way that
I don't think any of us

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:31.890
thought we could actually
do several months ago.

00:44:31.890 --> 00:44:34.290
Constant cancelling and reschedule

00:44:34.290 --> 00:44:38.490
and that just builds in lack
of productivity everywhere,

00:44:38.490 --> 00:44:40.770
but also just huge
disappointment and worry

00:44:40.770 --> 00:44:42.180
on behalf of the patients.

00:44:42.180 --> 00:44:44.280
And clinicians want to see patients.

00:44:44.280 --> 00:44:46.410
Nobody wants to be sitting around

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:48.600
unable to do what they need to do.

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:51.090
So it's very, very tense out here

00:44:51.090 --> 00:44:53.463
and people are very fatigued with it.

00:44:54.840 --> 00:44:56.580
I think what we can see,

00:44:56.580 --> 00:44:58.260
and you can see a bit
of this through summer

00:44:58.260 --> 00:44:59.910
and certainly in September,

00:44:59.910 --> 00:45:02.280
is when the NHS gets a run at this,

00:45:02.280 --> 00:45:03.990
it can make an impact

00:45:03.990 --> 00:45:05.670
in weeks where there isn't disruption,

00:45:05.670 --> 00:45:09.180
the list will fall, long waits will fall.

00:45:09.180 --> 00:45:12.270
And as you can see in
the data for last year

00:45:12.270 --> 00:45:14.490
when we had a steady run
at it over a few months,

00:45:14.490 --> 00:45:15.323
as Amanda said,

00:45:15.323 --> 00:45:17.520
we managed to stabilise the list

00:45:17.520 --> 00:45:20.520
nearly two years ahead of
time according to the plan.

00:45:20.520 --> 00:45:22.760
So I think that shows what the NHS can do.

00:45:24.060 --> 00:45:25.560
- [Richard] David, you were going to,

00:45:25.560 --> 00:45:27.270
have you had a question or a comment?

00:45:27.270 --> 00:45:28.770
- Yeah, thanks Jim.

00:45:28.770 --> 00:45:32.100
I just pay tribute to what
you've pulled out here,

00:45:32.100 --> 00:45:34.140
which is the improvements
that have taken place

00:45:34.140 --> 00:45:35.070
over those three months.

00:45:35.070 --> 00:45:37.680
I think that's really encouraging

00:45:37.680 --> 00:45:41.730
and plays off the back of Wol's question,

00:45:41.730 --> 00:45:43.830
but I just wonder at paragraph seven

00:45:43.830 --> 00:45:47.517
we talk about the waiting
list being at 7.68

00:45:47.517 --> 00:45:49.623
and it's a big figure that.

00:45:50.518 --> 00:45:52.800
And I'm beginning to
understand, sat behind that,

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:55.470
if you begin to segment that figure

00:45:55.470 --> 00:45:57.960
the numbers waiting for
surgery, for instance,

00:45:57.960 --> 00:46:00.087
is very different than in 7.68.

00:46:00.087 --> 00:46:02.850
And I just wondered
whether it might be helpful

00:46:02.850 --> 00:46:04.920
as we begin to present
these figures going forward,

00:46:04.920 --> 00:46:08.460
if we can actually just
break that figure down

00:46:08.460 --> 00:46:09.293
and segment it.

00:46:09.293 --> 00:46:11.340
I think it plays to the
point you're just making

00:46:11.340 --> 00:46:14.190
about being more precise
about what's going on there.

00:46:14.190 --> 00:46:16.410
- No, absolutely David. Absolutely.

00:46:16.410 --> 00:46:19.290
And in the initial part of this programme,

00:46:19.290 --> 00:46:23.670
when we first agreed, the
elective recovery plan,

00:46:23.670 --> 00:46:26.850
the long waits were very
surgically orientated.

00:46:26.850 --> 00:46:29.070
The vast majority of patients on the list,

00:46:29.070 --> 00:46:29.903
I've said before,

00:46:29.903 --> 00:46:31.860
are going to see a physician,

00:46:31.860 --> 00:46:33.630
have some diagnostic processes,

00:46:33.630 --> 00:46:36.090
and have things ruled out largely.

00:46:36.090 --> 00:46:37.470
So that is a very different thing

00:46:37.470 --> 00:46:39.030
and we do really need to try

00:46:39.030 --> 00:46:42.900
and illustrate that in a more nuanced way.

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:45.220
We're also trying to accelerate

00:46:46.609 --> 00:46:50.400
the first access for
patients to first outpatients

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:52.350
and then at least start a process

00:46:52.350 --> 00:46:55.170
so you're not on the list
with just complete uncertainty

00:46:55.170 --> 00:46:56.670
not being in a process as well.

00:46:56.670 --> 00:46:57.930
So over the next few months,

00:46:57.930 --> 00:47:00.450
we'll try and present the
data in different ways

00:47:00.450 --> 00:47:02.643
to give a different feel for that.

00:47:05.310 --> 00:47:06.870
- [Richard] Mark?

00:47:06.870 --> 00:47:08.880
- So prevention is always better than cure

00:47:08.880 --> 00:47:10.590
and it's worrying that England's lost

00:47:10.590 --> 00:47:12.513
its measles elimination status.

00:47:13.710 --> 00:47:15.650
Can someone tell us about it?

00:47:15.650 --> 00:47:17.910
It may be for Steve rather than you,

00:47:17.910 --> 00:47:20.640
sort of the whole issue with
the MMR Catchup campaign

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:22.260
and where we are with that?

00:47:22.260 --> 00:47:24.720
And maybe at the same
time to talk a bit about

00:47:24.720 --> 00:47:27.183
flu vaccination,
particularly in health stuff.

00:47:30.660 --> 00:47:32.310
- Yeah, thanks Mark. Happy to do that.

00:47:32.310 --> 00:47:35.820
So, routine childhood immunizations,

00:47:35.820 --> 00:47:38.190
which obviously includes MMR,

00:47:38.190 --> 00:47:40.950
have been under a sort
of progressive decline

00:47:40.950 --> 00:47:42.633
over the last 10 years.

00:47:43.650 --> 00:47:46.443
There are many, many factors behind that,

00:47:47.430 --> 00:47:51.930
but NHS England working with
UKHSA and the department

00:47:51.930 --> 00:47:56.610
have got both an outbreak
plan but also a recovery plan

00:47:56.610 --> 00:48:01.610
in terms of catch up for MMR
vaccination in particular.

00:48:03.120 --> 00:48:04.410
You'll be aware, colleagues will be aware,

00:48:04.410 --> 00:48:06.060
it's also very different
around the country.

00:48:06.060 --> 00:48:10.290
So uptake in London has
generally been lower

00:48:10.290 --> 00:48:11.610
than in other parts of the country

00:48:11.610 --> 00:48:16.290
and that is probably where the
greatest risk currently sits.

00:48:16.290 --> 00:48:19.020
So since the summer,

00:48:19.020 --> 00:48:21.690
NHS organisations in
London have been running

00:48:21.690 --> 00:48:24.420
a community-based outreach offer

00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:29.070
for both polio and MMR
that was run in two phases.

00:48:29.070 --> 00:48:33.150
One was a general offer
of a catchup vaccination

00:48:33.150 --> 00:48:34.320
in the routine settings,

00:48:34.320 --> 00:48:37.710
which is normally general practise.

00:48:37.710 --> 00:48:40.950
And then the second part of it has been

00:48:40.950 --> 00:48:43.830
in reach into schools and other venues

00:48:43.830 --> 00:48:46.620
where people have trust and confidence

00:48:46.620 --> 00:48:49.920
and go to with their children.

00:48:49.920 --> 00:48:53.520
So that continues to be
undertaken in London.

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:58.410
And then later this
year and into next year,

00:48:58.410 --> 00:49:00.780
there'll be a further national campaign,

00:49:00.780 --> 00:49:04.860
national call and recall run

00:49:04.860 --> 00:49:08.400
with some additional and
different provider models as well.

00:49:08.400 --> 00:49:13.110
So looking at where children
present and have contact with

00:49:13.110 --> 00:49:14.790
and trying to put vaccination offers

00:49:14.790 --> 00:49:18.090
in those places with the
professionals they meet.

00:49:18.090 --> 00:49:19.800
Just very briefly on flu,

00:49:19.800 --> 00:49:22.923
so I think as Amanda and Steve said,

00:49:24.960 --> 00:49:28.470
flu and Covid vaccination
are very important protection

00:49:28.470 --> 00:49:31.860
for healthcare workers both for themselves

00:49:31.860 --> 00:49:35.420
but also in terms of
protection for patients.

00:49:35.420 --> 00:49:37.320
So the campaign has started,

00:49:37.320 --> 00:49:38.820
it's quite early in the season

00:49:38.820 --> 00:49:40.470
so it's probably too early, Mark,

00:49:40.470 --> 00:49:44.490
to say where we think
uptake trends might be.

00:49:44.490 --> 00:49:48.120
But again, NHS organisations
up and down the country

00:49:48.120 --> 00:49:50.760
are doing all of the usual
high impact intervention.

00:49:50.760 --> 00:49:54.180
So particularly around peer
and roving vaccinations

00:49:54.180 --> 00:49:57.630
so that you vaccinate in
people's setting of work

00:49:57.630 --> 00:49:59.580
rather than asking them to go to a clinic

00:49:59.580 --> 00:50:02.310
that is often difficult to get
to because people are busy.

00:50:02.310 --> 00:50:05.490
So we're monitoring that
quite carefully and closely

00:50:05.490 --> 00:50:08.063
and we'll continue to do so
over the rest of the season.

00:50:09.330 --> 00:50:10.470
- Steve, thank you.

00:50:10.470 --> 00:50:13.470
And Jim, can you just remind
me on the vaccination centres,

00:50:13.470 --> 00:50:14.400
how many have we got?

00:50:14.400 --> 00:50:17.190
How many have we got open
now compared to last year?

00:50:17.190 --> 00:50:21.570
- For Covid there are about
four and a half thousand sites

00:50:21.570 --> 00:50:23.550
active and vaccinating.

00:50:23.550 --> 00:50:27.000
That compares to about
two to 3000 in the spring

00:50:27.000 --> 00:50:29.130
and about 3000 at last autumn.

00:50:29.130 --> 00:50:33.000
So it's a bigger, bigger
network and campaign this year.

00:50:33.000 --> 00:50:38.000
And flu is delivered in
usually around 11,000 settings.

00:50:38.580 --> 00:50:39.990
- Very good. I'm keen to get to Julian,

00:50:39.990 --> 00:50:42.563
but I can see Ruth wants to make a point.

00:50:42.563 --> 00:50:45.390
- Jim, if I may, on elective recovery,

00:50:45.390 --> 00:50:48.300
I know that there was a
piece of work over the summer

00:50:48.300 --> 00:50:49.800
about catch up for children,

00:50:49.800 --> 00:50:51.900
young people on the elective programme.

00:50:51.900 --> 00:50:53.700
I'm just urging us to continue

00:50:53.700 --> 00:50:57.248
to really focus on
catching up for children

00:50:57.248 --> 00:51:02.190
because it will affect their
lives much more severely.

00:51:02.190 --> 00:51:03.120
So it's really important.

00:51:03.120 --> 00:51:05.670
Thank you for the work you're doing.

00:51:05.670 --> 00:51:06.503
- Ruth. Thank you.

00:51:06.503 --> 00:51:08.010
And Jim, thank you very much.

00:51:08.010 --> 00:51:08.843
So Julian,

00:51:08.843 --> 00:51:10.920
can we turn to you on the
financial performance update?

00:51:10.920 --> 00:51:11.753
- Sure.

00:51:12.930 --> 00:51:15.480
The paper we published
provides the numbers

00:51:15.480 --> 00:51:17.940
up to the end of July, however,

00:51:17.940 --> 00:51:18.990
but I'm actually going
to talk about the numbers

00:51:18.990 --> 00:51:19.890
up to the end of August

00:51:19.890 --> 00:51:22.740
given that since we submitted the paper,

00:51:22.740 --> 00:51:23.730
we have had those,

00:51:23.730 --> 00:51:26.760
and it still tells a
broadly consistent story

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:28.173
to those in the paper.

00:51:29.850 --> 00:51:31.743
Up until the end of August,

00:51:32.850 --> 00:51:35.070
continuing a trend we have seen

00:51:35.070 --> 00:51:36.540
as we've gone through the year

00:51:36.540 --> 00:51:39.300
and in particular accelerating a bit

00:51:39.300 --> 00:51:44.300
as we have had combined consultant
and junior doctor strikes

00:51:44.580 --> 00:51:45.603
each month,

00:51:47.000 --> 00:51:51.390
we are seeing a total system
deficit up to the end of August

00:51:51.390 --> 00:51:56.280
of just over a billion
pounds as compared to plan.

00:51:56.280 --> 00:52:00.753
That is clearly a really sizable number.

00:52:02.850 --> 00:52:07.850
In the context of spending by systems,

00:52:08.160 --> 00:52:09.550
it actually represents

00:52:11.730 --> 00:52:16.730
just about 2% of spend up to this point.

00:52:18.210 --> 00:52:23.210
Now the dominant factor
behind that, I think,

00:52:23.400 --> 00:52:25.200
is the cost of strikes.

00:52:25.200 --> 00:52:30.200
Our estimates of the direct
financial cost of strikes

00:52:31.560 --> 00:52:34.350
by which I include both the extra money

00:52:34.350 --> 00:52:38.310
that is being spent on
strike days to pay people,

00:52:38.310 --> 00:52:43.260
to cover shifts in particular
in urgent emergency care,

00:52:47.010 --> 00:52:50.610
plus the impact of lost savings.

00:52:50.610 --> 00:52:53.580
Because just simply the kind
of disruption of strikes

00:52:53.580 --> 00:52:58.380
and the fact that management
clinical leadership time

00:52:58.380 --> 00:53:00.630
is focused on managing them.

00:53:00.630 --> 00:53:04.290
We currently value at 700 million pounds

00:53:04.290 --> 00:53:05.700
up until the end of August.

00:53:05.700 --> 00:53:08.160
So as you say, that is
the dominant factor.

00:53:08.160 --> 00:53:09.900
In addition,

00:53:09.900 --> 00:53:13.380
we estimate that we
have lost the equivalent

00:53:13.380 --> 00:53:16.890
of about 700 million
pounds if, in particular,

00:53:16.890 --> 00:53:20.160
elective activity that is both as a result

00:53:20.160 --> 00:53:24.090
of appointments that we
would've otherwise expected

00:53:24.090 --> 00:53:25.650
to have taken place,

00:53:25.650 --> 00:53:27.480
plus the fact that, again,

00:53:27.480 --> 00:53:29.250
we have been targeting productivity

00:53:29.250 --> 00:53:31.230
and efficiency improvements

00:53:31.230 --> 00:53:33.780
and change in transformation
which has not happened,

00:53:33.780 --> 00:53:38.070
which we think would've also
given us further activity.

00:53:38.070 --> 00:53:40.713
And that is really what is then driving,

00:53:42.927 --> 00:53:45.600
as Amanda and others have said,

00:53:45.600 --> 00:53:50.220
we've lost at least a million
appointments cancelled

00:53:50.220 --> 00:53:52.350
on those strike days.

00:53:52.350 --> 00:53:55.170
But in fact we think we've
probably lost more activity

00:53:55.170 --> 00:53:58.410
in the round, over the whole period.

00:53:58.410 --> 00:54:01.443
So that's the dominant factor.
It's not the only factor.

00:54:02.520 --> 00:54:06.810
We have seen also quite
significant increase

00:54:06.810 --> 00:54:10.950
in the cost of prescribing
in primary care.

00:54:10.950 --> 00:54:15.950
Now, that is influenced by three things.

00:54:16.680 --> 00:54:20.700
One, just the fact of
sort of global inflation

00:54:20.700 --> 00:54:22.920
has fed through into medicines prices,

00:54:22.920 --> 00:54:25.050
really did start to feed
through into the prices

00:54:25.050 --> 00:54:26.610
at the back end of last year

00:54:26.610 --> 00:54:30.153
and that has continued
through into this year.

00:54:31.980 --> 00:54:35.687
GPs are seeing a lot more
people than they were.

00:54:35.687 --> 00:54:40.470
It's a thing that is worth
repeating but as a result,

00:54:40.470 --> 00:54:44.430
they're seeing people who need care

00:54:44.430 --> 00:54:46.863
and therefore are being
prescribed medicines.

00:54:48.506 --> 00:54:50.130
And again, on the real plus side,

00:54:50.130 --> 00:54:52.770
it is also partly because
we have seen quite a growth

00:54:52.770 --> 00:54:55.230
in the prescription of DOACs,

00:54:55.230 --> 00:55:00.230
which we would anticipate
having substantial benefits

00:55:01.350 --> 00:55:03.780
for those who'd otherwise
have cardiovascular disease

00:55:03.780 --> 00:55:04.890
or stroke.

00:55:04.890 --> 00:55:06.690
And indeed we've seen quite a surge

00:55:06.690 --> 00:55:08.850
in the prescription of HRT.

00:55:08.850 --> 00:55:10.773
So those are the things which are,

00:55:11.880 --> 00:55:14.760
so there's some sort of
good news actually in there

00:55:14.760 --> 00:55:18.870
that we are seeing activity
that we want to take place,

00:55:18.870 --> 00:55:22.410
and in particular changes in prescribing

00:55:22.410 --> 00:55:25.500
that we have been actually
striving for are happening.

00:55:25.500 --> 00:55:27.240
But that is creating a financial pressure,

00:55:27.240 --> 00:55:28.530
and to give a sense of it,

00:55:28.530 --> 00:55:31.860
we're probably seeing prescribing spend

00:55:31.860 --> 00:55:35.032
grow by 45% annually rather than,

00:55:35.032 --> 00:55:38.130
for the last 10 or so years,

00:55:38.130 --> 00:55:41.490
it's been growing by about 1% annually.

00:55:41.490 --> 00:55:43.800
So that's the kind of scale of change.

00:55:43.800 --> 00:55:47.250
Now the third factor is that,

00:55:47.250 --> 00:55:49.350
notwithstanding the impact of strikes,

00:55:49.350 --> 00:55:54.180
I mean many ICBs and providers
have been doing actually

00:55:54.180 --> 00:55:56.940
an extraordinary job of
delivering the plans,

00:55:56.940 --> 00:56:01.230
including the proportion
of the kind of efficiency

00:56:01.230 --> 00:56:05.460
and saving plans that they
can given the circumstances,

00:56:05.460 --> 00:56:07.950
and therefore doing what
they said they would do.

00:56:07.950 --> 00:56:10.650
However, there are systems and providers

00:56:10.650 --> 00:56:13.410
who are struggling to do
what they said they would do,

00:56:13.410 --> 00:56:15.390
notwithstanding the impact of strikes.

00:56:15.390 --> 00:56:18.480
Now we are working through where,

00:56:18.480 --> 00:56:19.980
we think where that is the case,

00:56:19.980 --> 00:56:24.980
systematically to work
with ICBs, providers,

00:56:25.830 --> 00:56:27.480
individually and together,

00:56:27.480 --> 00:56:32.480
as systems to turn
those situations around.

00:56:32.790 --> 00:56:37.790
That includes providing
sourcing turnaround

00:56:40.380 --> 00:56:44.760
and improvement support
as we judge appropriate,

00:56:44.760 --> 00:56:49.350
and to make sure that we
are supporting those places

00:56:49.350 --> 00:56:53.160
to ensure that they
have the right personnel

00:56:53.160 --> 00:56:55.950
in sort of a non-exec and exec capacity.

00:56:55.950 --> 00:56:59.160
So, we will continue to do that.

00:56:59.160 --> 00:57:01.650
We are seeing signs of improvement.

00:57:01.650 --> 00:57:06.390
I mean I would say that we
are seeing agency spending

00:57:06.390 --> 00:57:10.350
coming down across the country.

00:57:10.350 --> 00:57:12.051
That is one of the things

00:57:12.051 --> 00:57:14.250
that we have really targeted this year

00:57:14.250 --> 00:57:18.810
and indeed we are seeing
provider spend has been flat

00:57:18.810 --> 00:57:20.970
and there is some signs
that it's actually beginning

00:57:20.970 --> 00:57:22.500
to reduce.

00:57:22.500 --> 00:57:23.820
I would say it's marginal,

00:57:23.820 --> 00:57:25.140
but there are some signs that

00:57:25.140 --> 00:57:27.840
as we've sort of got into the year,

00:57:27.840 --> 00:57:31.110
we are seeing people
despite all the challenges

00:57:31.110 --> 00:57:33.540
and despite the work they are doing

00:57:33.540 --> 00:57:36.870
to deliver the improvements in performance

00:57:36.870 --> 00:57:38.880
and sustaining quality

00:57:38.880 --> 00:57:43.860
of having appropriate control of costs.

00:57:43.860 --> 00:57:47.640
So we will continue to work
across those three dimensions.

00:57:47.640 --> 00:57:51.373
We are obviously still in
dialogue with government

00:57:51.373 --> 00:57:54.813
about how they want us to deal
with the impact of strikes.

00:57:56.070 --> 00:57:56.903
- Thank you.

00:57:56.903 --> 00:57:59.520
Amanda, is there anything
you would like to add as CO

00:57:59.520 --> 00:58:01.470
as you think about the
financial picture we face

00:58:01.470 --> 00:58:02.820
and as we head into winter?

00:58:04.260 --> 00:58:06.099
- Thank you, Richard.

00:58:06.099 --> 00:58:08.670
I mean, I think Julian's set
up the position very clearly.

00:58:08.670 --> 00:58:13.500
I guess where we have been
is very much encouraging,

00:58:13.500 --> 00:58:15.120
as Julian's just said,

00:58:15.120 --> 00:58:17.490
systems providers to continue to focus

00:58:17.490 --> 00:58:20.820
on delivery of their efficiency plans,

00:58:20.820 --> 00:58:22.530
sort of notwithstanding the disruption

00:58:22.530 --> 00:58:25.410
that's been described
because of strike action.

00:58:25.410 --> 00:58:28.320
But we have been encouraging
providers and systems

00:58:28.320 --> 00:58:31.050
to maintain elective activity

00:58:31.050 --> 00:58:34.542
and also to continue to
put in place those things

00:58:34.542 --> 00:58:37.832
which are going to give
us maximum resilience

00:58:37.832 --> 00:58:39.330
as we head into winter

00:58:39.330 --> 00:58:42.090
and also allow us to
make progress on things

00:58:42.090 --> 00:58:43.830
like our primary care recovery plan

00:58:43.830 --> 00:58:46.800
that we've talked about
at this board before.

00:58:46.800 --> 00:58:49.320
So, that's been where
we've been up to now.

00:58:49.320 --> 00:58:53.133
But I think as Julian
says, we clearly have had,

00:58:54.596 --> 00:58:55.429
which is very different to when

00:58:55.429 --> 00:58:56.490
we were in the planning process,

00:58:56.490 --> 00:58:59.610
a level of disruption to
the services in the NHS

00:58:59.610 --> 00:59:02.100
that just was not
anticipated at that time.

00:59:02.100 --> 00:59:04.230
And that has now put us in a position

00:59:04.230 --> 00:59:08.220
where I think we are aware
that at a local level,

00:59:08.220 --> 00:59:10.650
organisations are finding
it increasingly difficult

00:59:10.650 --> 00:59:12.600
to get that balance right.

00:59:12.600 --> 00:59:13.800
- Thank you. And Munir,

00:59:13.800 --> 00:59:15.940
I think you wanted to comment?

00:59:15.940 --> 00:59:16.977
- Thanks very much.

00:59:16.977 --> 00:59:21.093
The increase in prescriptions
by 45% is enormous.

00:59:23.130 --> 00:59:25.590
But NHS England and various
other published reports

00:59:25.590 --> 00:59:27.870
that as you increase medicines usage

00:59:27.870 --> 00:59:29.970
there's increasing wastage as well.

00:59:29.970 --> 00:59:32.130
Do we have any idea what
the degree of wastage is

00:59:32.130 --> 00:59:34.320
and what we're trying to
do to minimise our wastage?

00:59:34.320 --> 00:59:36.810
- I think the best estimate
is probably the work

00:59:36.810 --> 00:59:39.360
that Keith Ridge, our former
Chief Pharmacist, did,

00:59:39.360 --> 00:59:41.100
who published a report on this subject,

00:59:41.100 --> 00:59:42.848
I want to say last year,

00:59:42.848 --> 00:59:47.280
but I can't quite, two years ago now.

00:59:47.280 --> 00:59:50.220
Off the back of that actually
we have done various things

00:59:50.220 --> 00:59:54.420
like we have had incentives
in the GP contract

00:59:54.420 --> 00:59:58.980
and in the community pharmacy contract

00:59:58.980 --> 01:00:02.760
to sort of conduct
structured medicines reviews

01:00:02.760 --> 01:00:07.760
to make sure that people are
being appropriately prescribed.

01:00:07.950 --> 01:00:11.280
And we continue to work with ICBs

01:00:11.280 --> 01:00:14.310
to share data on prescribing patterns,

01:00:14.310 --> 01:00:15.630
and in fact I would call out the work

01:00:15.630 --> 01:00:17.940
that Ben Goldacre's team
do in actually publishing

01:00:17.940 --> 01:00:22.440
a lot of this data which
is available for use.

01:00:22.440 --> 01:00:25.680
So with them, we are working with ICBs

01:00:25.680 --> 01:00:28.020
to see are we using it,

01:00:28.020 --> 01:00:33.020
and are people talking to
regional and local teams

01:00:36.360 --> 01:00:39.440
in particular through the
regional pharmacy network.

01:00:39.440 --> 01:00:41.579
You wanted to say something?

01:00:41.579 --> 01:00:43.780
- There's an entire debate we
could have on that question,

01:00:43.780 --> 01:00:44.850
but broadly it comes under the umbrella

01:00:44.850 --> 01:00:47.070
of medicines optimization.

01:00:47.070 --> 01:00:50.070
So it includes ensuring,
as you would know Munir,

01:00:50.070 --> 01:00:53.580
that the right medicines are
prescribed at the right time

01:00:53.580 --> 01:00:54.780
for the right condition,

01:00:55.860 --> 01:00:58.170
through to ensuring that
medicines are reviewed

01:00:58.170 --> 01:00:59.430
on a regular basis to ensure

01:00:59.430 --> 01:01:00.888
that they're stopped at the right time

01:01:00.888 --> 01:01:03.090
if they're no longer required.

01:01:03.090 --> 01:01:05.730
And a whole host of technical
things in there as well.

01:01:05.730 --> 01:01:08.100
So there are big programmes
going on nationally

01:01:08.100 --> 01:01:10.860
and also a regional level around ensuring

01:01:10.860 --> 01:01:14.403
that the medicine usage is optimised.

01:01:18.420 --> 01:01:19.920
- Julian, thank you.

01:01:19.920 --> 01:01:23.463
Clearly it's a very
challenging financial picture.

01:01:26.250 --> 01:01:30.120
You commented on the higher
volume of primary care activity

01:01:30.120 --> 01:01:31.150
and the number of appointments and so on.

01:01:31.150 --> 01:01:35.190
I was just checking the operational deck.

01:01:35.190 --> 01:01:36.390
And actually what we do see there,

01:01:36.390 --> 01:01:40.890
we see an over 15% increase
in the last 12 months

01:01:40.890 --> 01:01:41.880
that we've recorded.

01:01:41.880 --> 01:01:46.080
So a 15% higher number of
appointments in the 12 months

01:01:46.080 --> 01:01:49.830
running up to March 19,
the pre-Covid period.

01:01:49.830 --> 01:01:51.870
So we're seeing an increase in volume,

01:01:51.870 --> 01:01:54.120
which then drives that
higher intervention,

01:01:54.120 --> 01:01:55.950
higher prescription.

01:01:55.950 --> 01:01:58.266
But clearly you also referenced, I think,

01:01:58.266 --> 01:02:00.810
on prescription and medicine costs,

01:02:00.810 --> 01:02:03.000
the impact of inflation coming through.

01:02:03.000 --> 01:02:04.740
But industrial action, from your comment,

01:02:04.740 --> 01:02:07.732
is the main driver of the
challenges we're seeing

01:02:07.732 --> 01:02:09.270
in the financial picture.

01:02:09.270 --> 01:02:12.990
I think that as a board,
led by you, Julian,

01:02:12.990 --> 01:02:15.330
we are very aware of
these financial pressures

01:02:15.330 --> 01:02:17.010
and of our obligations,

01:02:17.010 --> 01:02:21.720
but importantly we remain
in guidance to our systems

01:02:21.720 --> 01:02:24.990
and to our trusts when we are asking them

01:02:24.990 --> 01:02:26.850
to put in place the capacity to deal with

01:02:26.850 --> 01:02:28.850
the winter surge to look after patients.

01:02:31.350 --> 01:02:33.840
Anything you want to-
- That's fine.

01:02:33.840 --> 01:02:34.950
- Thank you very much.

01:02:34.950 --> 01:02:38.160
Okay, let's move on.

01:02:38.160 --> 01:02:41.823
So Tom, Tom Cahill, welcome.

01:02:42.990 --> 01:02:44.490
You are the National Director

01:02:44.490 --> 01:02:46.980
for Learning Disability and Autism

01:02:46.980 --> 01:02:50.919
is your formal title and you hosted for us

01:02:50.919 --> 01:02:52.440
just prior to this meeting

01:02:52.440 --> 01:02:55.300
a really good, actually

01:02:57.030 --> 01:03:02.030
broad ranging but training
session on Oliver McGowan.

01:03:02.100 --> 01:03:04.050
So the Oliver McGowan training,

01:03:04.050 --> 01:03:07.590
which I personally found really helpful,

01:03:07.590 --> 01:03:08.700
raises my awareness,

01:03:08.700 --> 01:03:11.010
makes me think about my interaction.

01:03:11.010 --> 01:03:13.530
I'm sure board colleagues
would've felt the same.

01:03:13.530 --> 01:03:16.530
And I'm sure they'll comment.

01:03:16.530 --> 01:03:19.293
But actually in particular
to Jeanie North,

01:03:21.403 --> 01:03:23.310
and to Vicki Livingston Thompson,

01:03:23.310 --> 01:03:26.640
and to Tim Heaven who led
that training session.

01:03:26.640 --> 01:03:28.530
I'd also like to record
the board's thanks.

01:03:28.530 --> 01:03:33.530
Actually was a really good
mandatory training intervention.

01:03:33.660 --> 01:03:34.560
But Tom, over to you,

01:03:34.560 --> 01:03:36.630
there's a paper here on
learning disability and autism.

01:03:36.630 --> 01:03:38.940
It follows up some comments in the summer

01:03:38.940 --> 01:03:42.400
about outcomes that we
see in the health service

01:03:44.820 --> 01:03:47.370
particularly on reduced
life expectancy often

01:03:47.370 --> 01:03:50.280
for those with learning disabilities.

01:03:50.280 --> 01:03:51.113
- Thank you.

01:03:51.113 --> 01:03:53.450
Can I be begin by commending the board

01:03:53.450 --> 01:03:55.020
in just having gone through the training,

01:03:55.020 --> 01:03:56.190
Oliver McGowan training,

01:03:56.190 --> 01:03:57.930
and then sitting and listening firsthand

01:03:57.930 --> 01:04:02.930
to Jeanie and Tim and the
sort of lives they live

01:04:03.519 --> 01:04:05.970
and the things that we need to do

01:04:05.970 --> 01:04:08.790
to help them live those lives.

01:04:08.790 --> 01:04:10.770
So really, really commend
the board on that one.

01:04:10.770 --> 01:04:14.010
Also commend the board on
having this agenda item.

01:04:14.010 --> 01:04:15.510
I'm a bit nervous about all the questions,

01:04:15.510 --> 01:04:20.370
but delighted that the board
is prioritising this group.

01:04:20.370 --> 01:04:21.930
Just a bit of reminder about context.

01:04:21.930 --> 01:04:24.120
I know I'm here to talk
about health outcomes

01:04:24.120 --> 01:04:24.953
and that element about it,

01:04:24.953 --> 01:04:26.610
but just reminder of a
little bit of context

01:04:26.610 --> 01:04:27.960
about the programme.

01:04:27.960 --> 01:04:29.785
We estimate there's
about 1.5 million people

01:04:29.785 --> 01:04:32.040
with a learning disability in the UK,

01:04:32.040 --> 01:04:34.287
I think between 700,000
and a million people

01:04:34.287 --> 01:04:35.310
who are autistic.

01:04:35.310 --> 01:04:39.210
So it gives you an idea
of the scale of the issue.

01:04:39.210 --> 01:04:41.760
The programme had been
operating around NHSE

01:04:41.760 --> 01:04:46.140
since about 2015, refreshed in 2019.

01:04:46.140 --> 01:04:47.670
And there are just three priority areas

01:04:47.670 --> 01:04:49.710
that I'll just put in the room.

01:04:49.710 --> 01:04:52.710
The first one is about
people who are in hospital

01:04:52.710 --> 01:04:53.760
and we know that we have

01:04:53.760 --> 01:04:56.430
about 2000 people who are in hospital,

01:04:56.430 --> 01:05:00.390
and one is making sure there
is appropriate quality.

01:05:00.390 --> 01:05:02.767
Two, getting people out who
shouldn't be in hospital

01:05:02.767 --> 01:05:04.560
and there's some really good work there,

01:05:04.560 --> 01:05:06.990
but also providing real
alternatives in the community.

01:05:06.990 --> 01:05:09.254
And I think in the last three years,

01:05:09.254 --> 01:05:11.670
NHSE have spent about 121 million

01:05:11.670 --> 01:05:14.070
providing different opportunities
for people to stay out.

01:05:14.070 --> 01:05:15.930
And one of them was key workers,

01:05:15.930 --> 01:05:17.100
which Amanda and I saw last week,

01:05:17.100 --> 01:05:18.780
which is really good.

01:05:18.780 --> 01:05:20.910
The second priority is health outcomes.

01:05:20.910 --> 01:05:23.910
And the anchor point for
that is annual health checks,

01:05:23.910 --> 01:05:25.020
but much more,

01:05:25.020 --> 01:05:26.910
really helping people
with learning disability

01:05:26.910 --> 01:05:30.360
and autistic people get a better
deal, get better outcomes.

01:05:30.360 --> 01:05:31.193
And then the third one,

01:05:31.193 --> 01:05:33.690
which is a probably newer, more 2020,

01:05:33.690 --> 01:05:37.140
was the Autism Programme.

01:05:37.140 --> 01:05:40.290
And whilst it is aligned
to the learning disability,

01:05:40.290 --> 01:05:42.120
it is a standalone programme.

01:05:42.120 --> 01:05:43.560
Many people who are autistic

01:05:43.560 --> 01:05:45.600
do not have a learning disability

01:05:45.600 --> 01:05:47.010
and that has a priority

01:05:47.010 --> 01:05:50.070
about getting good
practise around pathways.

01:05:50.070 --> 01:05:51.600
We know about the waiting list,

01:05:51.600 --> 01:05:53.370
Making sure environments,

01:05:53.370 --> 01:05:55.920
the NHS environments
particularly, are adjusted now,

01:05:55.920 --> 01:05:57.870
to think about people who are autistic.

01:05:57.870 --> 01:05:59.730
And just remember Jeanie in that room

01:05:59.730 --> 01:06:03.930
and what she said about noise,
touch, all of those things.

01:06:03.930 --> 01:06:05.100
Really important.

01:06:05.100 --> 01:06:08.160
And health inequalities is a similar issue

01:06:08.160 --> 01:06:11.520
for people who are autistic.

01:06:11.520 --> 01:06:13.950
So the papers there,
just a couple of points.

01:06:13.950 --> 01:06:17.460
You've seen the information
from the last LeDeR review,

01:06:17.460 --> 01:06:18.660
we're about to see the new one,

01:06:18.660 --> 01:06:20.040
and I can't say too much about that today,

01:06:20.040 --> 01:06:22.260
but we've seen the last one.

01:06:22.260 --> 01:06:25.440
Very stark about life expectancy.

01:06:25.440 --> 01:06:27.360
But the most critical
one is probably perhaps

01:06:27.360 --> 01:06:29.340
is the avoidable deaths.

01:06:29.340 --> 01:06:30.570
And the word avoidable is the one

01:06:30.570 --> 01:06:33.840
that we need to grab on, comparatively,

01:06:33.840 --> 01:06:38.840
and then compounded by Race
and Health Observatory report,

01:06:39.270 --> 01:06:41.100
which talks about people
with learning disability

01:06:41.100 --> 01:06:42.510
from an ethnic background,

01:06:42.510 --> 01:06:46.920
which really talks even
further about life expectancy.

01:06:46.920 --> 01:06:48.300
And really profound,

01:06:48.300 --> 01:06:51.030
and I'm really glad that
you're wanting me back here

01:06:51.030 --> 01:06:53.043
to talk about it and prioritise that.

01:06:54.480 --> 01:06:57.930
We know if you look at some
of the data that's coming out

01:06:57.930 --> 01:07:01.590
around respiratory conditions, diabetes,

01:07:01.590 --> 01:07:03.720
hypertension, cancer,

01:07:03.720 --> 01:07:05.520
and if you just think
about the conversation

01:07:05.520 --> 01:07:07.200
we've just had about waiting lists,

01:07:07.200 --> 01:07:08.970
there will be people who've
got a learning disability

01:07:08.970 --> 01:07:11.130
and autism in those waiting lists.

01:07:11.130 --> 01:07:13.140
And we are talking to
colleagues about, hang on,

01:07:13.140 --> 01:07:14.313
can we do some stuff?

01:07:15.180 --> 01:07:17.067
But it's not all doom and gloom.

01:07:17.067 --> 01:07:18.330
And just to pick up,

01:07:18.330 --> 01:07:20.970
so we've got learning disability
improvement standards,

01:07:20.970 --> 01:07:22.680
which Ruth and our team are leading,

01:07:22.680 --> 01:07:25.770
which all acute hospitals are working to

01:07:25.770 --> 01:07:27.720
and trying to comply with investors,

01:07:27.720 --> 01:07:29.363
we're seeing really good stuff there.

01:07:29.363 --> 01:07:32.160
There's a couple of
examples of acute hospitals

01:07:32.160 --> 01:07:33.930
who are going the extra
mile, and there are many,

01:07:33.930 --> 01:07:35.760
but I'll just pick out Calderdale,

01:07:35.760 --> 01:07:37.440
who are going through their waiting list

01:07:37.440 --> 01:07:39.630
and fast tracking people
with a learning disability.

01:07:39.630 --> 01:07:40.590
Leicester Royal Infirmary,

01:07:40.590 --> 01:07:43.320
fantastic stuff around disability,

01:07:43.320 --> 01:07:46.740
sending taxis out to get
people into appointments

01:07:46.740 --> 01:07:48.390
and recruiting people
with a learning disability

01:07:48.390 --> 01:07:51.090
and also appraising their waiting lists.

01:07:51.090 --> 01:07:52.230
And I visited the Whittington,

01:07:52.230 --> 01:07:54.510
who are doing what I would call pod videos

01:07:54.510 --> 01:07:56.490
for people who are coming into hospital,

01:07:56.490 --> 01:07:59.460
videos run by people with a
learning disability and autism

01:07:59.460 --> 01:08:01.860
to show to people who are
about to come into hospital,

01:08:01.860 --> 01:08:04.170
whether there's outpatients,

01:08:04.170 --> 01:08:07.500
really innovative stuff
but not mind blowing.

01:08:07.500 --> 01:08:09.690
So really, really good to see that.

01:08:09.690 --> 01:08:12.651
If you see then, about the
Oliver McGowan training,

01:08:12.651 --> 01:08:15.030
and we can't endorse that enough

01:08:15.030 --> 01:08:16.377
and we will continue to do it,

01:08:16.377 --> 01:08:17.940
but some really good figures,

01:08:17.940 --> 01:08:22.940
2.5 million launches on
the web since November 22.

01:08:25.080 --> 01:08:28.770
750,000 take up of part
one of the training

01:08:28.770 --> 01:08:31.110
and the online training,
which you've just done.

01:08:31.110 --> 01:08:33.093
And there's 200 train the trainers

01:08:33.093 --> 01:08:34.310
already in that programme.

01:08:34.310 --> 01:08:35.430
So really good work

01:08:35.430 --> 01:08:36.780
and you'll probably have
to take your hat off

01:08:36.780 --> 01:08:39.870
to Paula McGowan and her
family for driving that.

01:08:39.870 --> 01:08:43.110
And Amanda and I met with her last week,

01:08:43.110 --> 01:08:47.310
and she's a spirited
individual who we will support

01:08:47.310 --> 01:08:48.780
as best we can.

01:08:48.780 --> 01:08:50.010
You can see then on the paper,

01:08:50.010 --> 01:08:52.950
there's some of the key
headlines that we have

01:08:52.950 --> 01:08:55.470
around digital flag,

01:08:55.470 --> 01:08:58.350
and I think it's a game
changer if we can get this done

01:08:58.350 --> 01:08:59.183
and we're looking for '24.

01:08:59.183 --> 01:09:01.350
And you've heard the
conversation earlier about

01:09:01.350 --> 01:09:04.740
how do you know if somebody
needs a reasonable adjustment.

01:09:04.740 --> 01:09:06.810
If the digital flag works properly,

01:09:06.810 --> 01:09:09.780
we put the data in properly
and we use it properly

01:09:09.780 --> 01:09:11.730
it is a game changer about telling.

01:09:11.730 --> 01:09:13.320
Now it's for disability
in the broader sense,

01:09:13.320 --> 01:09:15.750
but learning disability
and autism are the pilot

01:09:15.750 --> 01:09:16.583
and driving that.

01:09:16.583 --> 01:09:18.720
You can see where we're doing
improvement in primary care

01:09:18.720 --> 01:09:23.190
and secondary prevention
around Covid flu vaccinations,

01:09:23.190 --> 01:09:24.780
supporting obesity and diabetes,

01:09:24.780 --> 01:09:26.790
which are particular issues

01:09:26.790 --> 01:09:29.370
for people with a learning disability.

01:09:29.370 --> 01:09:34.370
Cancer screening and CVD
and respiratory activities.

01:09:34.500 --> 01:09:36.690
The annual health check's
really important to us.

01:09:36.690 --> 01:09:37.740
We've seen an uptake.

01:09:37.740 --> 01:09:41.100
We have a target for 75% by '24,

01:09:41.100 --> 01:09:42.990
we're already at 78%.

01:09:42.990 --> 01:09:47.550
Really big push last year to achieve that.

01:09:47.550 --> 01:09:49.140
Just a cautionary number,

01:09:49.140 --> 01:09:51.990
350,000 are properly on the GP register.

01:09:51.990 --> 01:09:56.340
So we increased by 12,000 last
year, which is really good.

01:09:56.340 --> 01:09:58.080
But if you think about 1.5 million,

01:09:58.080 --> 01:09:58.913
there's still more to do

01:09:58.913 --> 01:10:01.500
and you'd never expect the
1.5 million on the register.

01:10:01.500 --> 01:10:04.110
But we know we've got
more to do to get that.

01:10:04.110 --> 01:10:07.230
And we're now starting
autism health checks.

01:10:07.230 --> 01:10:08.700
We're really doing some pilots about

01:10:08.700 --> 01:10:10.323
what that will look like.

01:10:11.310 --> 01:10:15.180
Clearly LeDeR is, the next report is out,

01:10:15.180 --> 01:10:18.900
but we continue to plan
and to drive the learning

01:10:18.900 --> 01:10:21.300
and we have significant learning overview

01:10:21.300 --> 01:10:24.270
and we often think about,

01:10:24.270 --> 01:10:25.230
do we need to keep doing it

01:10:25.230 --> 01:10:27.030
because we know the data already.

01:10:27.030 --> 01:10:28.500
We keep learning new data.

01:10:28.500 --> 01:10:31.680
Cancer is something that
we hadn't quite seen before

01:10:31.680 --> 01:10:33.390
for people with a learning disability

01:10:33.390 --> 01:10:35.490
as an avoidable cause of death.

01:10:35.490 --> 01:10:38.493
And so we're now pushing
on the screening on that.

01:10:38.493 --> 01:10:40.473
So we continue to learn from that.

01:10:41.400 --> 01:10:44.880
STOMP STAMP, really
important around medication.

01:10:44.880 --> 01:10:47.010
And what we know from the
Oliver McGowan training,

01:10:47.010 --> 01:10:49.200
it's a reminder about the importance of,

01:10:49.200 --> 01:10:50.970
so it's not just about over-medicating.

01:10:50.970 --> 01:10:52.940
Where perhaps STOMP started from

01:10:52.940 --> 01:10:54.060
at the beginning of the day,

01:10:54.060 --> 01:10:55.167
it's much more sophisticated now.

01:10:55.167 --> 01:10:58.680
Are we medicating people that
helps them in their best way

01:10:58.680 --> 01:11:01.740
and wherever possible avoiding medication?

01:11:01.740 --> 01:11:04.920
Autism waiting time's
obviously a challenge for us.

01:11:04.920 --> 01:11:06.750
We are seeing an increase in activity

01:11:06.750 --> 01:11:08.760
as much as we are seeing an increase

01:11:08.760 --> 01:11:10.950
in the numbers being referred,

01:11:10.950 --> 01:11:14.746
but we are seeing an increase
in the numbers being seen

01:11:14.746 --> 01:11:17.625
but it is still a challenge.

01:11:17.625 --> 01:11:22.260
And clearly this is going to
be an issue for workforce.

01:11:22.260 --> 01:11:24.720
We have challenges of workforce

01:11:24.720 --> 01:11:27.150
that I think anyone in
the room could talk about,

01:11:27.150 --> 01:11:28.800
and it's not just the nursing workforce

01:11:28.800 --> 01:11:30.660
it's really about the care sector.

01:11:30.660 --> 01:11:33.090
Have we got people who can
take people at a hospital

01:11:33.090 --> 01:11:34.090
and look after them?

01:11:35.994 --> 01:11:39.243
And that begins to move beyond
just the health outcomes.

01:11:41.370 --> 01:11:44.290
So I think that's
probably given you a sense

01:11:45.608 --> 01:11:47.730
of what we're doing.

01:11:47.730 --> 01:11:50.070
I think the ask I would have is

01:11:50.070 --> 01:11:54.840
we continue to profile this,
we continue to champion it.

01:11:54.840 --> 01:11:58.230
We are talking to ICSs about
what they can do locally,

01:11:58.230 --> 01:12:00.120
what can they do in their cancer networks,

01:12:00.120 --> 01:12:01.797
what can they do in their
respiratory networks.

01:12:01.797 --> 01:12:05.220
And we would encourage that
to continue wherever possible.

01:12:05.220 --> 01:12:07.530
There's something about
just making this real,

01:12:07.530 --> 01:12:09.660
shining a light on particulars,

01:12:09.660 --> 01:12:11.313
it is not too big to deal with.

01:12:12.180 --> 01:12:14.010
If you look at it as an
entirety, it probably is,

01:12:14.010 --> 01:12:15.579
but it is not too big.

01:12:15.579 --> 01:12:17.137
There are things we can do
and we're already seeing them

01:12:17.137 --> 01:12:20.310
and we're already seeing
hospitals doing it.

01:12:20.310 --> 01:12:22.140
We're already seeing
primary care doing it.

01:12:22.140 --> 01:12:24.753
So, we just got to keep
this momentum going.

01:12:25.590 --> 01:12:28.320
- Tom, thank you very
much for those comments

01:12:28.320 --> 01:12:30.030
and also for a very good paper.

01:12:30.030 --> 01:12:31.920
Can I just open it up for comments?

01:12:31.920 --> 01:12:33.693
David and then Ruth and then Mary.

01:12:35.490 --> 01:12:36.480
- Thanks, Richard.

01:12:36.480 --> 01:12:37.730
Tom, thank you very much.

01:12:38.790 --> 01:12:42.300
And thanks for the positive
presentation of the issue,

01:12:42.300 --> 01:12:43.560
and certainly Richard,

01:12:43.560 --> 01:12:46.800
I'd really make a case
for this coming back

01:12:46.800 --> 01:12:50.400
on a regular basis so we can
actually do what Tom's asking,

01:12:50.400 --> 01:12:52.920
which is keep an eye on this.

01:12:52.920 --> 01:12:56.617
I think the avoidable
deaths make the point

01:12:59.700 --> 01:13:01.646
about how much more there is to do.

01:13:01.646 --> 01:13:03.600
There's some quite
distressing figures in there

01:13:03.600 --> 01:13:05.970
and we touched on that at
the last board meeting,

01:13:05.970 --> 01:13:10.020
but you're right to pull out
what progress has been made.

01:13:10.020 --> 01:13:11.640
There's two issues though

01:13:11.640 --> 01:13:14.910
that I'm not looking
for an answer for now,

01:13:14.910 --> 01:13:18.270
but there's two issues that
just pulled me up short

01:13:18.270 --> 01:13:20.100
when I was reading the report.

01:13:20.100 --> 01:13:23.550
One is, do we have the right
people in the right place?

01:13:23.550 --> 01:13:25.920
It's your point about people
with learning disabilities

01:13:25.920 --> 01:13:27.240
being in institutions,

01:13:27.240 --> 01:13:29.583
which we know are not
the best place for them.

01:13:30.540 --> 01:13:34.110
And I don't know what
the benchmark is anymore

01:13:34.110 --> 01:13:36.720
on how many we think are
there inappropriately

01:13:36.720 --> 01:13:38.700
and how many should be moving out,

01:13:38.700 --> 01:13:43.050
on whether we've got traction
and visibility on that.

01:13:43.050 --> 01:13:45.330
You might, but I'm not
sure it is to the board.

01:13:45.330 --> 01:13:48.000
and therefore I would make an ask

01:13:48.000 --> 01:13:49.380
that in subsequent reporting

01:13:49.380 --> 01:13:51.210
we can have greater visibility on that

01:13:51.210 --> 01:13:54.720
because there were some of these

01:13:54.720 --> 01:13:57.240
quite distressing human
rights cases come from

01:13:57.240 --> 01:13:59.730
of people with incredibly complex needs

01:13:59.730 --> 01:14:03.090
being kept in isolation,
et cetera, et cetera.

01:14:03.090 --> 01:14:05.660
And I do think given what we had

01:14:05.660 --> 01:14:07.620
in Manchester earlier this year,

01:14:07.620 --> 01:14:11.700
there's a real need to keep that visible

01:14:11.700 --> 01:14:14.640
because there is really an issue

01:14:14.640 --> 01:14:17.820
about who champions this group of people.

01:14:17.820 --> 01:14:19.563
So, that's my first point.

01:14:21.120 --> 01:14:22.623
The Oliver McGowan training,

01:14:24.690 --> 01:14:27.060
I've been around services for a long time,

01:14:27.060 --> 01:14:30.420
but listening to Oliver's
mother tell the story

01:14:30.420 --> 01:14:32.040
at the beginning of that training,

01:14:32.040 --> 01:14:35.880
I found very, very powerful indeed.

01:14:35.880 --> 01:14:40.140
And I want to pick up
on the medication point,

01:14:40.140 --> 01:14:42.720
the STOMP training makes
a point about medication.

01:14:42.720 --> 01:14:47.160
This is Munir's area better than mine

01:14:47.160 --> 01:14:50.190
but I do worry whether
we're putting the onus

01:14:50.190 --> 01:14:53.160
around making sure people are being given

01:14:53.160 --> 01:14:55.290
the right medication on families

01:14:55.290 --> 01:14:58.110
and not on the clinicians
who do the prescribing.

01:14:58.110 --> 01:14:59.550
And my question is,

01:14:59.550 --> 01:15:03.600
what is the challenge to the
prescribers of the medication

01:15:03.600 --> 01:15:06.420
to get that right as
distinct from the families

01:15:06.420 --> 01:15:07.857
having to champion this?

01:15:07.857 --> 01:15:11.640
And as I say, we touched
on this over lunch, Tom,

01:15:11.640 --> 01:15:13.200
so I'm not looking for an answer,

01:15:13.200 --> 01:15:17.010
but I think it is something
that we need to come back on

01:15:17.010 --> 01:15:22.010
because Oliver's story
is a very vivid example

01:15:22.110 --> 01:15:23.370
of if that's not got right,

01:15:23.370 --> 01:15:24.870
what the consequences are.

01:15:24.870 --> 01:15:29.870
And I do think it's important
to keep that in vision.

01:15:30.120 --> 01:15:30.953
Thank you.

01:15:30.953 --> 01:15:32.970
- [Richard] David, we'll get the comments,

01:15:32.970 --> 01:15:35.975
if you want to try and capture
them and then deal with them.

01:15:35.975 --> 01:15:37.170
So we'll go, Ruth, I think you were next.

01:15:37.170 --> 01:15:39.000
Then I'm coming to Mary I think.

01:15:39.000 --> 01:15:40.530
- Yes, just two points for me.

01:15:40.530 --> 01:15:45.530
So the Oliver McGowan
training was just a reminder

01:15:46.290 --> 01:15:48.000
about listening to patients,

01:15:48.000 --> 01:15:50.310
listening to families and of course,

01:15:50.310 --> 01:15:51.540
we'll talk about that in a moment

01:15:51.540 --> 01:15:53.670
when we come on to maternity.

01:15:53.670 --> 01:15:56.220
So great tribute to Oliver

01:15:56.220 --> 01:15:59.753
and I'm very happy personally
to have been supporting this

01:15:59.753 --> 01:16:00.813
as well.

01:16:01.800 --> 01:16:03.180
So thank you Paula,

01:16:03.180 --> 01:16:05.613
and thank you Tom for
your enthusiasm of it.

01:16:06.660 --> 01:16:10.950
With regard to workforce,
'cause you've mentioned it,

01:16:10.950 --> 01:16:13.520
whilst we've got more nurses now this year

01:16:13.520 --> 01:16:15.000
than we had last year,

01:16:15.000 --> 01:16:17.070
we've got the most ever
healthcare support workers

01:16:17.070 --> 01:16:18.573
we've ever had ever.

01:16:19.920 --> 01:16:21.030
Mental health nursing,

01:16:21.030 --> 01:16:23.400
and learning disability
nursing in particular,

01:16:23.400 --> 01:16:25.590
is the most challenged,

01:16:25.590 --> 01:16:28.500
and we have got some
international recruits

01:16:28.500 --> 01:16:30.370
from all over the world,

01:16:30.370 --> 01:16:34.470
but only tiny, tiny, tiny
numbers for learning disability.

01:16:34.470 --> 01:16:37.620
I think it was only three in
the last couple of months.

01:16:37.620 --> 01:16:40.350
So this is where the
long-term workforce plan,

01:16:40.350 --> 01:16:44.250
David and Navina, really
comes into into play,

01:16:44.250 --> 01:16:48.180
how we maximise every bit of that ambition

01:16:48.180 --> 01:16:51.300
to deliver those increases in supply.

01:16:51.300 --> 01:16:55.410
So for me the long-term
workforce plan is key

01:16:55.410 --> 01:16:59.370
to how we deliver some of
the service improvements

01:16:59.370 --> 01:17:01.203
for this population.

01:17:02.160 --> 01:17:02.993
- [Richard] Thank you.

01:17:02.993 --> 01:17:05.460
So Mary and actually and then Wol also,

01:17:05.460 --> 01:17:07.697
and then I'll get you Tom to comment,

01:17:07.697 --> 01:17:08.853
and then Amanda.

01:17:09.720 --> 01:17:11.940
Okay, so we're going to
go Mary first I think.

01:17:11.940 --> 01:17:12.780
- Thank you very much,

01:17:12.780 --> 01:17:15.390
and thank you for the presentation,

01:17:15.390 --> 01:17:19.380
which I think does show that
we are really getting a grip

01:17:19.380 --> 01:17:24.380
on the challenges that were
for so long hidden actually.

01:17:24.480 --> 01:17:27.000
So I'm delighted that they've been raised

01:17:27.000 --> 01:17:30.777
to such a sensible level
for us all to focus on.

01:17:30.777 --> 01:17:33.480
But I wanted to look at item six

01:17:33.480 --> 01:17:36.840
about reducing reliance on
mental health inpatient care

01:17:36.840 --> 01:17:40.470
in particular because for
me there are two things.

01:17:40.470 --> 01:17:44.580
People with learning
disability and autistic people,

01:17:44.580 --> 01:17:48.030
you have described as
suffering, a proportion,

01:17:48.030 --> 01:17:50.700
from cancer and respiratory disease.

01:17:50.700 --> 01:17:52.290
And the same will be true,

01:17:52.290 --> 01:17:55.920
some of those group will
have mental health problems

01:17:55.920 --> 01:17:58.980
and somehow we've got to ensure

01:17:58.980 --> 01:18:02.520
that they get equal and swift treatment

01:18:02.520 --> 01:18:05.550
rather than when they have
a mental health problem,

01:18:05.550 --> 01:18:08.820
be admitted and then
stay for years and years.

01:18:08.820 --> 01:18:11.460
And I really wanted to ask about

01:18:11.460 --> 01:18:13.143
how we are going to get the people

01:18:13.143 --> 01:18:16.080
that have been in mental health facilities

01:18:16.080 --> 01:18:19.890
out more quickly looking at the fact,

01:18:19.890 --> 01:18:22.620
and I've highlighted this, Tom,

01:18:22.620 --> 01:18:27.620
that 2023/24 sees the continuation

01:18:27.930 --> 01:18:30.330
of the National Housing Capital programme

01:18:30.330 --> 01:18:34.380
with an annual allocation
of 13 million pounds.

01:18:34.380 --> 01:18:37.050
Now the average facility,

01:18:37.050 --> 01:18:39.750
if you're going to put
somebody in individually

01:18:39.750 --> 01:18:41.430
with specialists support,

01:18:41.430 --> 01:18:43.590
and not all of those
people will need that,

01:18:43.590 --> 01:18:45.390
will cost half a million.

01:18:45.390 --> 01:18:47.400
So by my visioning,

01:18:47.400 --> 01:18:52.400
we've probably got 26 new
homes for people a year.

01:18:53.400 --> 01:18:56.130
And yet how many have we
still got in hospitals

01:18:56.130 --> 01:18:57.150
that we'd like to get out?

01:18:57.150 --> 01:18:58.800
That's a really core question for me.

01:18:58.800 --> 01:19:00.325
- So what I'm going to do,

01:19:00.325 --> 01:19:02.220
I'm going to change my original tactic.

01:19:02.220 --> 01:19:03.450
And I'd like you to,

01:19:03.450 --> 01:19:05.250
if you want to pick up and respond

01:19:05.250 --> 01:19:06.720
to David and to Ruth and to Mary

01:19:06.720 --> 01:19:09.060
and then I'll bring Wol and Amanda.

01:19:09.060 --> 01:19:11.010
Well, now I'm changing my tactic again.

01:19:15.210 --> 01:19:16.043
- Sorry.

01:19:16.043 --> 01:19:16.876
No, it's just to follow up on that,

01:19:16.876 --> 01:19:17.760
because actually I was interested

01:19:17.760 --> 01:19:18.930
in local authority partnerships

01:19:18.930 --> 01:19:19.860
and the work you're doing there.

01:19:19.860 --> 01:19:24.030
Because of course we are about
one part of the equation.

01:19:24.030 --> 01:19:25.470
There's an equally important equation

01:19:25.470 --> 01:19:27.230
which we know is the local authority

01:19:27.230 --> 01:19:28.630
is under incredible pressure

01:19:29.670 --> 01:19:31.080
in terms of how they support,

01:19:31.080 --> 01:19:33.150
particularly in education for example,

01:19:33.150 --> 01:19:35.400
and supporting people with
learning disabilities,

01:19:35.400 --> 01:19:36.870
housing, et cetera.

01:19:36.870 --> 01:19:38.580
And that sort of coming
together to make sure

01:19:38.580 --> 01:19:40.440
we have a balanced
approach is really crucial.

01:19:40.440 --> 01:19:42.088
So sorry just a comment.

01:19:42.088 --> 01:19:42.921
- [Richard] Tom?

01:19:42.921 --> 01:19:45.540
- So firstly the gist
of the paper, if I can,

01:19:45.540 --> 01:19:47.460
just was about health outcomes.

01:19:47.460 --> 01:19:49.500
So you're not seeing a lot in their beds.

01:19:49.500 --> 01:19:51.120
We just put some bed numbers in there

01:19:51.120 --> 01:19:52.890
and begin to talk about
people in hospital,

01:19:52.890 --> 01:19:54.780
but just bear with me a second.

01:19:54.780 --> 01:19:56.043
We do know the numbers.

01:19:58.080 --> 01:20:00.690
Two years ago we went through,

01:20:00.690 --> 01:20:03.130
when we reviewed every
individual in hospital

01:20:04.590 --> 01:20:06.660
during that period

01:20:06.660 --> 01:20:10.523
and our estimates at the
time were that about 41%,

01:20:11.719 --> 01:20:14.730
about 40-41% probably shouldn't be there

01:20:14.730 --> 01:20:17.670
or should be receiving
care in another setting.

01:20:17.670 --> 01:20:19.740
So we've got a good set,

01:20:19.740 --> 01:20:23.760
we've broken this number down by ICS

01:20:23.760 --> 01:20:26.430
and we're working really
closely with the ICSs.

01:20:26.430 --> 01:20:27.780
So part of my thinking is,

01:20:27.780 --> 01:20:29.400
and you've just heard me saying,

01:20:29.400 --> 01:20:32.640
we make the problems too big
so that nobody can get them.

01:20:32.640 --> 01:20:33.930
We have an understanding now,

01:20:33.930 --> 01:20:37.200
a close understanding of
everybody who's in hospital

01:20:37.200 --> 01:20:40.620
and can talk in detail about them.

01:20:40.620 --> 01:20:44.520
You are right around the
challenges around capital

01:20:44.520 --> 01:20:46.710
because we know the housing is about,

01:20:46.710 --> 01:20:48.050
I think it's about 12%,

01:20:48.050 --> 01:20:52.290
13% of those who can't, for
a reason, for not going out.

01:20:52.290 --> 01:20:56.760
But I wouldn't want to underplay
the 13 or the 15 million

01:20:56.760 --> 01:21:00.120
I think over the space of two
years that placed 80 people.

01:21:00.120 --> 01:21:03.060
Okay, because we think
big bang all the time.

01:21:03.060 --> 01:21:05.100
A lot of people need modifications.

01:21:05.100 --> 01:21:07.630
So we use that money really well

01:21:08.610 --> 01:21:10.110
anytime we sell property and recycle,

01:21:10.110 --> 01:21:11.790
so it is really good but not enough.

01:21:11.790 --> 01:21:14.610
And one of the things we're
pushing on on social care

01:21:14.610 --> 01:21:16.320
and we've got good
relationship with social care

01:21:16.320 --> 01:21:19.710
and housing and the minister about,

01:21:19.710 --> 01:21:21.960
okay, what can we do more about housing?

01:21:21.960 --> 01:21:24.780
What are councils doing
locally around housing

01:21:24.780 --> 01:21:27.000
that are thinking ahead?

01:21:27.000 --> 01:21:30.877
Because we have discharged 80% of people

01:21:30.877 --> 01:21:33.393
who were in hospital in
2015 are no longer there.

01:21:34.260 --> 01:21:36.210
So, there's the really important thing.

01:21:36.210 --> 01:21:37.170
So things have moved,

01:21:37.170 --> 01:21:39.300
I'm not trying to sell a
really positive thing here.

01:21:39.300 --> 01:21:42.240
There's still around 2000
people in the hospital,

01:21:42.240 --> 01:21:44.823
200 kids, 1800 adults.

01:21:46.200 --> 01:21:48.030
But what we are seeing is
a different group of people

01:21:48.030 --> 01:21:50.180
coming in for hospital
and not for as long.

01:21:51.153 --> 01:21:54.480
And we're very clear hospital is not bad

01:21:54.480 --> 01:21:58.020
for the people who need it for
a particular period of time.

01:21:58.020 --> 01:22:00.840
And that's really important
that we understand that.

01:22:00.840 --> 01:22:03.363
Our target is still 30 per million,

01:22:04.950 --> 01:22:07.860
which is really what
we're trying to drive,

01:22:07.860 --> 01:22:10.590
but we've really been
trying to use the data,

01:22:10.590 --> 01:22:11.970
understand what it's telling us.

01:22:11.970 --> 01:22:14.550
So we're seeing a lot more autistic people

01:22:14.550 --> 01:22:16.920
coming into hospital who
need mental health support,

01:22:16.920 --> 01:22:18.360
going into slightly different hospitals,

01:22:18.360 --> 01:22:20.310
going into mental health hospitals to stay

01:22:20.310 --> 01:22:21.240
but not going in for as long.

01:22:21.240 --> 01:22:24.270
So we have to think differently
about what's the solution.

01:22:24.270 --> 01:22:25.500
So I'm more than happy

01:22:25.500 --> 01:22:28.140
to even spend some time with you offline,

01:22:28.140 --> 01:22:30.633
but I didn't come with all that data.

01:22:31.740 --> 01:22:34.800
I don't want to paint a rosy picture

01:22:34.800 --> 01:22:36.720
because there's many families that'll say

01:22:36.720 --> 01:22:39.690
my son and my daughter or
my sister is in hospital

01:22:39.690 --> 01:22:40.983
for far too long.

01:22:40.983 --> 01:22:41.970
And we would agree with that.

01:22:41.970 --> 01:22:45.060
Equally, I can point
to some fantastic work

01:22:45.060 --> 01:22:48.660
where people have been in
hospital for 20, 25, 26, 27 years

01:22:48.660 --> 01:22:50.280
and now in their new homes.

01:22:50.280 --> 01:22:51.570
We will continue to do that.

01:22:51.570 --> 01:22:54.270
There's no doubt about
that, we will drive that.

01:22:54.270 --> 01:22:56.913
We will need more capital
to do that, of course.

01:22:58.230 --> 01:23:00.300
- [Richard] Thank you. So
Amanda, do you want to?

01:23:00.300 --> 01:23:01.920
- Oh yeah, so thank you.

01:23:01.920 --> 01:23:05.700
So actually what I wanted
to do, if it's okay,

01:23:05.700 --> 01:23:09.690
just to go back to the visit Tom and I did

01:23:09.690 --> 01:23:11.940
at the end of last week to Black Country

01:23:11.940 --> 01:23:14.100
because I think sometimes with
these sorts of conversations,

01:23:14.100 --> 01:23:16.290
of course it's absolutely
right that we do focus

01:23:16.290 --> 01:23:18.660
on where things are not
working as well as they can

01:23:18.660 --> 01:23:20.250
and the LeDeR reviews,

01:23:20.250 --> 01:23:24.180
they really do highlight
exactly the size and scale

01:23:24.180 --> 01:23:27.990
of the problem that we need
to collectively lean into.

01:23:27.990 --> 01:23:30.300
And we are, and I just
want, actually if I may,

01:23:30.300 --> 01:23:33.030
just to pay tribute to Tom and his team

01:23:33.030 --> 01:23:36.990
who are leading with
such incredible passion

01:23:36.990 --> 01:23:39.300
as well as an impact,

01:23:39.300 --> 01:23:41.670
but also my colleagues who've leaned into,

01:23:41.670 --> 01:23:44.910
so Navina, your work with Paula to develop

01:23:44.910 --> 01:23:45.953
the Oliver McGowan training, for example,

01:23:45.953 --> 01:23:48.060
I think has been hugely important.

01:23:48.060 --> 01:23:50.850
Ruth, your and your team's work as well.

01:23:50.850 --> 01:23:53.280
But what we saw in the Black Country,

01:23:53.280 --> 01:23:56.520
which was really extraordinary was,

01:23:56.520 --> 01:23:59.190
what the potential is
when you get it right.

01:23:59.190 --> 01:24:00.630
And actually talking to,

01:24:00.630 --> 01:24:02.753
I mean Tom and I came back
on the train together,

01:24:04.684 --> 01:24:06.870
talking to Matty and the
relationship he had with Duncan,

01:24:06.870 --> 01:24:07.710
his key worker,

01:24:07.710 --> 01:24:10.890
it was Nathan and the
relationship he had with Dave,

01:24:10.890 --> 01:24:11.723
his carer,

01:24:11.723 --> 01:24:13.620
it was Ellie and the
relationship she had with Shay,

01:24:13.620 --> 01:24:14.490
who was her carer,

01:24:14.490 --> 01:24:17.070
and the way that those
young people were able

01:24:17.070 --> 01:24:22.070
to talk about their experiences
and where they are now

01:24:22.980 --> 01:24:25.830
and their hopes and
aspirations for the future.

01:24:25.830 --> 01:24:28.230
And that for me was, in a nutshell,

01:24:28.230 --> 01:24:31.170
what we've just talked about
with Paula McGowan's work

01:24:31.170 --> 01:24:33.540
and the Oliver McGowan training.

01:24:33.540 --> 01:24:34.500
Ask, listen, and do.

01:24:34.500 --> 01:24:36.635
That's what they have done.

01:24:36.635 --> 01:24:38.730
They have worked with those young people

01:24:38.730 --> 01:24:41.040
to understand what mattered to them

01:24:41.040 --> 01:24:44.070
and to develop and design
and deliver services

01:24:44.070 --> 01:24:47.040
in a absolute example of partnership,

01:24:47.040 --> 01:24:48.390
the way we've just been talking about it.

01:24:48.390 --> 01:24:49.920
This is partnership with
the voluntary sector,

01:24:49.920 --> 01:24:51.450
it's partnership with the private sector,

01:24:51.450 --> 01:24:54.630
it's partnership between
commissioners and providers,

01:24:54.630 --> 01:24:58.980
it's between local
authorities and the NHS.

01:24:58.980 --> 01:25:02.100
And they have made a difference,

01:25:02.100 --> 01:25:06.450
incalculable difference to the
lives of those young people.

01:25:06.450 --> 01:25:07.980
And the thing that I think I want to

01:25:07.980 --> 01:25:10.230
just encourage us to continue to do

01:25:10.230 --> 01:25:13.020
is to have that balance
between all the things

01:25:13.020 --> 01:25:15.270
we know we need to do
and we need to get right,

01:25:15.270 --> 01:25:19.433
but actually working with and
promoting and highlighting

01:25:19.433 --> 01:25:22.140
the examples of when it goes right.

01:25:22.140 --> 01:25:24.450
Because that's what we
want to see everywhere.

01:25:24.450 --> 01:25:25.860
And the bit for me, Tom,

01:25:25.860 --> 01:25:28.800
is what I particularly want
to talk about in this bit,

01:25:28.800 --> 01:25:31.721
Mary you just talked
about, it's the interfaces.

01:25:31.721 --> 01:25:33.630
So I think that we picked
up quite a lot on Friday,

01:25:33.630 --> 01:25:34.463
didn't we?

01:25:34.463 --> 01:25:36.030
It's great if you can
do it without a service

01:25:36.030 --> 01:25:38.880
which is designed for people
who have specific needs,

01:25:38.880 --> 01:25:40.080
but what happens when their lives

01:25:40.080 --> 01:25:41.220
touch other parts of the system,

01:25:41.220 --> 01:25:43.560
which they do every single day.

01:25:43.560 --> 01:25:46.530
So some of the encouragement
that I think you've given

01:25:46.530 --> 01:25:49.140
at the moment to
colleagues in acute trusts,

01:25:49.140 --> 01:25:50.340
to colleagues in primary care,

01:25:50.340 --> 01:25:52.230
to colleagues in community trusts,

01:25:52.230 --> 01:25:54.720
which training is such a huge part of it,

01:25:54.720 --> 01:25:55.590
but it's more than that.

01:25:55.590 --> 01:25:57.990
It's the practical actions
that you've just talked about

01:25:57.990 --> 01:25:59.970
with whether it's
Calderdale and Huddersfield

01:25:59.970 --> 01:26:01.170
or Sarah-Jane, you were just sharing

01:26:01.170 --> 01:26:03.300
some of the practical
reasonable adjustments

01:26:03.300 --> 01:26:04.808
that you were making when
you were chief executive

01:26:04.808 --> 01:26:08.010
just a short while ago.

01:26:08.010 --> 01:26:09.780
And the more that we can, I think,

01:26:09.780 --> 01:26:12.030
think across boundaries with this,

01:26:12.030 --> 01:26:14.823
the bigger impact we
will make collectively.

01:26:16.020 --> 01:26:16.853
- Amanda, thank you.

01:26:16.853 --> 01:26:17.700
- With your permission, Chair,

01:26:17.700 --> 01:26:20.280
one reflection I've got is a key worker,

01:26:20.280 --> 01:26:21.510
key worker that has strategy,

01:26:21.510 --> 01:26:22.560
it's been really good.

01:26:22.560 --> 01:26:25.173
They help people navigate their lives.

01:26:26.130 --> 01:26:28.233
One of them is a civil engineer.

01:26:29.550 --> 01:26:31.380
- Very good, John.

01:26:31.380 --> 01:26:35.760
John, thank you for bringing
that topic to the board

01:26:35.760 --> 01:26:38.490
and we will be bringing you back actually.

01:26:38.490 --> 01:26:39.690
So I think to David's point,

01:26:39.690 --> 01:26:41.520
it would be really important I think

01:26:41.520 --> 01:26:44.130
to keep this as a regular item.

01:26:44.130 --> 01:26:45.420
So thank you.

01:26:45.420 --> 01:26:47.430
Can we go onto item number six,

01:26:47.430 --> 01:26:48.960
which is a three year delivery plan

01:26:48.960 --> 01:26:51.150
for maternity and neonatal services.

01:26:51.150 --> 01:26:52.170
And Ruth, I think you're leading,

01:26:52.170 --> 01:26:54.427
but you've got Duncan and
Kate and Ngozi with you.

01:26:54.427 --> 01:26:59.190
Would you introduce them
and then the floor is yours.

01:26:59.190 --> 01:27:00.556
- Thank you very much.

01:27:00.556 --> 01:27:01.760
So just want to go back

01:27:01.760 --> 01:27:03.900
to what Richard and Amanda started with,

01:27:03.900 --> 01:27:06.510
which is acknowledging the awful events

01:27:06.510 --> 01:27:09.033
that have taken place
at Countess of Chester.

01:27:10.110 --> 01:27:12.180
Can't imagine the pain and the suffering

01:27:12.180 --> 01:27:14.970
that those parents and
families will go through

01:27:14.970 --> 01:27:16.770
and have been going through.

01:27:16.770 --> 01:27:20.700
Steve and I went up to
Countess of Chester last week

01:27:20.700 --> 01:27:22.102
to visit the staff

01:27:22.102 --> 01:27:27.102
and it was a truly humbling
experience to be there.

01:27:27.210 --> 01:27:32.103
So starting now then I shall
talk about neonatal first.

01:27:33.570 --> 01:27:38.570
We are investing and continue
to invest in neonatal capacity

01:27:38.880 --> 01:27:41.670
both in terms of cots in the right places

01:27:41.670 --> 01:27:43.650
but also the workforce.

01:27:43.650 --> 01:27:46.170
And I'm delighted also that we've now got

01:27:46.170 --> 01:27:49.413
our very first National
Neonatal Nurse Lead, Louise,

01:27:50.310 --> 01:27:52.890
and we are in the midst of recruiting

01:27:52.890 --> 01:27:54.900
our first ever National Clinical Director

01:27:54.900 --> 01:27:57.450
for Neonatal Critical Care.

01:27:57.450 --> 01:28:00.120
Ngozi is going to speak in a moment

01:28:00.120 --> 01:28:02.430
about some of the work on data.

01:28:02.430 --> 01:28:05.250
You'll know that of course
Bill Kirkup's review

01:28:05.250 --> 01:28:08.580
that was published October
the 15th last year.

01:28:08.580 --> 01:28:10.350
So just coming up to year.

01:28:10.350 --> 01:28:15.117
One of its key recommendations
was around signalling,

01:28:16.560 --> 01:28:19.200
the future signalling and the data.

01:28:19.200 --> 01:28:20.853
So that's really important.

01:28:22.380 --> 01:28:24.273
So now on to maternity,

01:28:25.203 --> 01:28:27.420
I'm sure you'll know that,

01:28:27.420 --> 01:28:31.380
that since Sir David's retirement,

01:28:31.380 --> 01:28:34.170
I'm now the SRO for the Independent Review

01:28:34.170 --> 01:28:36.900
of Maternity Services in Nottingham.

01:28:36.900 --> 01:28:38.100
The terms of reference,

01:28:38.100 --> 01:28:40.860
and I know the board through
the Quality committee

01:28:40.860 --> 01:28:43.180
have approved our recommendation to them

01:28:44.138 --> 01:28:48.360
that we move from opt in to opt out.

01:28:48.360 --> 01:28:53.360
So that means that as long
as the criteria outline

01:28:53.460 --> 01:28:54.630
in the terms of reference,

01:28:54.630 --> 01:28:59.630
then cases will be seen unless
of course families opt out.

01:29:00.300 --> 01:29:02.040
Donna Ockenden, the Review Chair,

01:29:02.040 --> 01:29:03.990
has written now to all of those families.

01:29:03.990 --> 01:29:08.283
So it now feels as if it
is really started underway.

01:29:09.840 --> 01:29:12.930
I'm pleased today to
announce that we've got

01:29:12.930 --> 01:29:16.320
a new Chair of our Maternity
and Neonatal board.

01:29:16.320 --> 01:29:19.350
Sam Allen who's the Chief Executive

01:29:19.350 --> 01:29:22.500
of the North East and North Cumbria ICB.

01:29:22.500 --> 01:29:26.400
She will take over from
what Sarah-Jane left

01:29:26.400 --> 01:29:27.390
some months ago now

01:29:27.390 --> 01:29:29.100
and I've been doing in the interim period.

01:29:29.100 --> 01:29:32.160
So I'm delighted that Sam is doing it.

01:29:32.160 --> 01:29:34.140
A forceful leader,

01:29:34.140 --> 01:29:38.010
a kind, compassionate
leader just like Sarah-Jane.

01:29:38.010 --> 01:29:41.460
But also important I
think to really stress

01:29:41.460 --> 01:29:43.890
the roles and the
responsibilities and influence

01:29:43.890 --> 01:29:46.530
that ICBs have in this agenda.

01:29:46.530 --> 01:29:48.945
So that to me is really important.

01:29:48.945 --> 01:29:51.210
I'd like to say, although
he is not here on record,

01:29:51.210 --> 01:29:53.730
our thanks to Matthew
Jolly who's coming away,

01:29:53.730 --> 01:29:56.970
stepping away now as the
National Clinical Director.

01:29:56.970 --> 01:30:00.180
He's done it for eight years,
I think he's done his stint.

01:30:00.180 --> 01:30:02.280
So my sincere thank you to Matthew

01:30:02.280 --> 01:30:05.070
and welcome to Kate Brintworth,

01:30:05.070 --> 01:30:07.080
our new Chief Midwifery Officer.

01:30:07.080 --> 01:30:10.530
Both Sam and Kate's appointment.

01:30:10.530 --> 01:30:12.540
We've involved service users,

01:30:12.540 --> 01:30:14.163
they've had the casting vote,

01:30:15.060 --> 01:30:17.070
and that has been really important

01:30:17.070 --> 01:30:18.840
about listening to families

01:30:18.840 --> 01:30:22.263
and putting them at the
heart of what we are doing.

01:30:23.610 --> 01:30:25.713
So just in terms of context,

01:30:27.900 --> 01:30:32.700
in England, a baby is
born every 55 seconds.

01:30:32.700 --> 01:30:35.340
There is a caesarian delivery of a baby

01:30:35.340 --> 01:30:38.880
every two minutes and 35 seconds.

01:30:38.880 --> 01:30:42.000
This demonstrates the
scale of which maternity

01:30:42.000 --> 01:30:45.870
and neonatal services are
across the whole of England.

01:30:45.870 --> 01:30:49.680
And equally the importance
of delivering against

01:30:49.680 --> 01:30:53.670
this three year maternity
and neonatal plan.

01:30:53.670 --> 01:30:56.940
I'm going to hand over
to Kate, then Ngozi,

01:30:56.940 --> 01:30:58.950
and then Duncan, and then open the floor.

01:30:58.950 --> 01:31:00.060
Thank you, Chair.

01:31:00.060 --> 01:31:01.860
- Thank you, Ruth.

01:31:01.860 --> 01:31:03.930
Good afternoon everyone.

01:31:03.930 --> 01:31:06.840
I'm really struck by the
theme that has come out

01:31:06.840 --> 01:31:08.490
from everything we've discussed

01:31:08.490 --> 01:31:10.290
about listening to women and families,

01:31:10.290 --> 01:31:11.793
the people we care for.

01:31:11.793 --> 01:31:14.184
And it has to be our
priority if we're going

01:31:14.184 --> 01:31:15.293
to improve safety

01:31:15.293 --> 01:31:18.810
and that isn't just in a
clinical setting at the bedside

01:31:18.810 --> 01:31:20.970
or wherever we are caring for people,

01:31:20.970 --> 01:31:22.830
but that's also in how we manage,

01:31:22.830 --> 01:31:24.660
design and oversee our services.

01:31:24.660 --> 01:31:28.470
They absolutely need to be
integral to everything we do

01:31:28.470 --> 01:31:30.720
and certainly in our
own team we are really,

01:31:30.720 --> 01:31:33.570
really privileged to have
a number of service users

01:31:33.570 --> 01:31:35.730
who challenge us, support us,

01:31:35.730 --> 01:31:38.940
and help us with every
decision that we make.

01:31:38.940 --> 01:31:40.680
And that's exactly how it should be

01:31:40.680 --> 01:31:43.590
if we're going to set the
right tone and right direction

01:31:43.590 --> 01:31:45.840
and have services that
actually meet the needs

01:31:45.840 --> 01:31:47.823
and are safe for those we care for.

01:31:48.810 --> 01:31:51.270
We are replicating that at
our regional level as well.

01:31:51.270 --> 01:31:52.890
We're supporting our regional colleagues

01:31:52.890 --> 01:31:54.900
with how they're working
with service users

01:31:54.900 --> 01:31:57.840
and producing guidance for ICS and trusts

01:31:57.840 --> 01:32:00.690
to set that expectation that really,

01:32:00.690 --> 01:32:02.940
you have to listen to
those that you care for.

01:32:02.940 --> 01:32:04.380
You have to work with them.

01:32:04.380 --> 01:32:07.500
And that needs to be both
for maternity and neonatal.

01:32:07.500 --> 01:32:09.480
The funding infrastructure and resources

01:32:09.480 --> 01:32:11.850
have to be in place for both.

01:32:11.850 --> 01:32:15.000
And part of that really
is about culture change.

01:32:15.000 --> 01:32:16.980
I think we would all agree that culture

01:32:16.980 --> 01:32:19.140
is at the heart of what
makes a safe service.

01:32:19.140 --> 01:32:22.350
And again, it's been referenced
frequently this afternoon.

01:32:22.350 --> 01:32:23.730
And it's why we've instigated

01:32:23.730 --> 01:32:25.770
a programme of leadership and support

01:32:25.770 --> 01:32:28.710
for every single maternity
service in the country.

01:32:28.710 --> 01:32:32.370
And so far, 98 have
started that programme.

01:32:32.370 --> 01:32:34.590
It brings together maternity, neonatal,

01:32:34.590 --> 01:32:37.800
general leadership and
our obstetric colleagues

01:32:37.800 --> 01:32:40.860
to really think about how
do you set a safe culture

01:32:40.860 --> 01:32:41.693
in your service?

01:32:41.693 --> 01:32:44.070
How do you make it feel a
safe and welcoming place

01:32:44.070 --> 01:32:45.450
to come to work?

01:32:45.450 --> 01:32:47.040
How are you making sure you're listening

01:32:47.040 --> 01:32:48.603
to those that you care for?

01:32:49.560 --> 01:32:52.767
All services will have
completed that by September 24

01:32:52.767 --> 01:32:55.740
and all will have started
it by Christmas this year.

01:32:55.740 --> 01:32:59.220
And the feedback we're having
is absolutely phenomenal.

01:32:59.220 --> 01:33:01.200
But we also need to
start with our students

01:33:01.200 --> 01:33:03.720
because we need to get it
right right from the beginning.

01:33:03.720 --> 01:33:04.710
And so we have,

01:33:04.710 --> 01:33:07.500
we are just about to
launch our Safe Learning

01:33:07.500 --> 01:33:09.300
and Environmental charter,

01:33:09.300 --> 01:33:11.100
which really sets the standards out

01:33:11.100 --> 01:33:13.440
of what it means to be
welcomed into the service

01:33:13.440 --> 01:33:15.210
from the very beginning of your career,

01:33:15.210 --> 01:33:18.090
made to feel like you're
important as a student,

01:33:18.090 --> 01:33:21.210
that what you do and say right
from the start of your career

01:33:21.210 --> 01:33:23.133
is set out in the right way.

01:33:24.639 --> 01:33:25.770
And we're also investing

01:33:25.770 --> 01:33:28.620
in professional midwifery advocate support

01:33:28.620 --> 01:33:31.410
to look after the health
and wellbeing staff.

01:33:31.410 --> 01:33:34.290
Jim talked about the stress
that our staff are under

01:33:34.290 --> 01:33:35.880
and I think everyone
would acknowledge that.

01:33:35.880 --> 01:33:38.250
So putting in that health
and wellbeing guidance

01:33:38.250 --> 01:33:40.233
is really, really important.

01:33:41.070 --> 01:33:43.710
But we need to listen to
all our staff and all voices

01:33:43.710 --> 01:33:45.300
and we are aware that we,

01:33:45.300 --> 01:33:48.923
in our richly diverse
people that we care for

01:33:48.923 --> 01:33:50.310
and our staff group,

01:33:50.310 --> 01:33:52.590
we know that there are
inequities in the outcomes

01:33:52.590 --> 01:33:54.780
and that's something
that we have to tackle.

01:33:54.780 --> 01:33:57.390
We know that perinatal mortality outcomes

01:33:57.390 --> 01:33:59.670
are not the same for all ethnic groups

01:33:59.670 --> 01:34:01.530
and that is not acceptable.

01:34:01.530 --> 01:34:05.700
We completely know that we
have a lot of work to do.

01:34:05.700 --> 01:34:08.820
Similarly, our staff talk
about their poor experiences,

01:34:08.820 --> 01:34:11.910
but we also know that
when we invest in support

01:34:11.910 --> 01:34:13.290
we can make a difference.

01:34:13.290 --> 01:34:16.440
So initiatives like
Capital Midwife in London

01:34:16.440 --> 01:34:18.180
have supported staff,

01:34:18.180 --> 01:34:20.550
40% of staff who undertook that programme,

01:34:20.550 --> 01:34:23.790
have moved into promotion from
ethnic minority backgrounds.

01:34:23.790 --> 01:34:26.520
That is making a tangible difference.

01:34:26.520 --> 01:34:27.353
And similarly,

01:34:27.353 --> 01:34:30.870
we are starting to see a
shift in asking our ICSs,

01:34:30.870 --> 01:34:32.730
they willingly stepped into the space,

01:34:32.730 --> 01:34:34.650
to look at the equality and equity plans

01:34:34.650 --> 01:34:36.150
that they needed to put in place

01:34:36.150 --> 01:34:38.550
to meet the needs of their population.

01:34:38.550 --> 01:34:41.700
And we are starting to see
the green shoots emerging.

01:34:41.700 --> 01:34:42.960
We are all very familiar

01:34:42.960 --> 01:34:46.500
with the harrowing "five
times more" statistic.

01:34:46.500 --> 01:34:49.083
The latest data from Embrace tells us

01:34:49.083 --> 01:34:51.330
that this is now just below four.

01:34:51.330 --> 01:34:53.160
That's not a reason to be complacent,

01:34:53.160 --> 01:34:54.990
but it shows that change is possible

01:34:54.990 --> 01:34:57.900
and we can head in the right direction.

01:34:57.900 --> 01:34:59.580
And now I'd like to hand over to Ngozi

01:34:59.580 --> 01:35:01.662
to talk more about data.

01:35:01.662 --> 01:35:03.363
- Thank you. Thank you, Kate.

01:35:04.647 --> 01:35:05.940
As Ruth said,

01:35:05.940 --> 01:35:08.400
we're now approaching the
first year anniversary

01:35:08.400 --> 01:35:11.520
of the publication of
the review of East Kent.

01:35:11.520 --> 01:35:14.460
One of the really strong recommendations

01:35:14.460 --> 01:35:17.010
was the need to develop the ability

01:35:17.010 --> 01:35:20.310
to spot signals of concern
amongst all the data

01:35:20.310 --> 01:35:22.443
that we have in neonatal services.

01:35:23.670 --> 01:35:25.650
This data's already available,

01:35:25.650 --> 01:35:26.850
but what we need to be able to do

01:35:26.850 --> 01:35:29.566
is identify trends and outliers

01:35:29.566 --> 01:35:32.673
because we have quite a lot of data.

01:35:34.866 --> 01:35:39.060
This has led to the establishment
of an outcomes group,

01:35:39.060 --> 01:35:42.333
which is chaired by my
colleague Edile Murdoch.

01:35:43.320 --> 01:35:45.900
This group has now developed
a set of outcome measures

01:35:45.900 --> 01:35:48.630
which we hope will be
helpful in identifying

01:35:48.630 --> 01:35:51.750
these trends of concern.

01:35:51.750 --> 01:35:56.250
It includes a set of
metrics which providers,

01:35:56.250 --> 01:36:00.690
regional teams, and a national
view will be able to use

01:36:00.690 --> 01:36:03.090
to be able to undertake comparisons.

01:36:03.090 --> 01:36:06.480
And I feel that this is a real
step in the right direction

01:36:06.480 --> 01:36:09.360
and I'm really pleased
about this development.

01:36:09.360 --> 01:36:12.330
However, today we've heard about data

01:36:12.330 --> 01:36:14.490
from almost everybody that's spoken.

01:36:14.490 --> 01:36:16.260
Tom talked about data,

01:36:16.260 --> 01:36:18.480
and the Chief Executive
in her opening speech

01:36:18.480 --> 01:36:20.040
talked about data as well.

01:36:20.040 --> 01:36:22.593
And we know that there is
perhaps more to be done.

01:36:24.240 --> 01:36:27.484
Vin, it's been your portfolio
to look at data as well,

01:36:27.484 --> 01:36:28.440
and I was really pleased to hear about

01:36:28.440 --> 01:36:30.900
the work that you'll be undertaking.

01:36:30.900 --> 01:36:33.210
But we know that there needs
to be more focus on data

01:36:33.210 --> 01:36:35.640
within neonatal maternity services

01:36:35.640 --> 01:36:37.560
to try and link up the data that we have

01:36:37.560 --> 01:36:41.988
between maternity and
neonatal to try and make more,

01:36:41.988 --> 01:36:45.180
to try and understand it a bit better.

01:36:45.180 --> 01:36:47.670
We also need to improve the data linkages

01:36:47.670 --> 01:36:51.690
between neonatal services
and the rest of EPR services

01:36:51.690 --> 01:36:53.370
within hospitals.

01:36:53.370 --> 01:36:56.820
Currently we have fragmented
EPR services in some instances

01:36:56.820 --> 01:37:00.060
and this doesn't help us
to understand the data

01:37:00.060 --> 01:37:01.680
that we already have.

01:37:01.680 --> 01:37:06.680
So we need to find a way of
getting good data intelligence

01:37:06.900 --> 01:37:10.023
and good access to information
about our services.

01:37:11.040 --> 01:37:13.890
Thank you so much. And I'll
now hand over to Duncan.

01:37:13.890 --> 01:37:14.723
- Thanks, Ngozi.

01:37:14.723 --> 01:37:17.730
So I think just also to,

01:37:17.730 --> 01:37:19.740
you'll see in the paper
that we've provided,

01:37:19.740 --> 01:37:21.510
an update on the CQC inspections.

01:37:21.510 --> 01:37:23.280
I think it's really
important that we listen

01:37:23.280 --> 01:37:25.830
to what we're hearing
out of those inspections.

01:37:25.830 --> 01:37:27.450
And you will know from,

01:37:27.450 --> 01:37:29.820
when we pulled together the
three year delivery plan

01:37:29.820 --> 01:37:32.640
and presented that back to
the board six months ago,

01:37:32.640 --> 01:37:34.890
we used the CQC inspections

01:37:34.890 --> 01:37:37.470
as part of pulling that plan together.

01:37:37.470 --> 01:37:39.660
But we continue to look at
the learning out of that.

01:37:39.660 --> 01:37:41.640
And you'll see in here
there's also a focus now

01:37:41.640 --> 01:37:44.850
on triage and all of our regional teams

01:37:44.850 --> 01:37:48.600
are providing support to trusts
and systems around triage.

01:37:48.600 --> 01:37:51.480
We're waiting for some
further best practise guidance

01:37:51.480 --> 01:37:53.430
which is being produced by the RCOG,

01:37:53.430 --> 01:37:56.100
who we clearly work very closely with.

01:37:56.100 --> 01:37:58.140
And there's been some good
examples, for example,

01:37:58.140 --> 01:38:00.450
in the southeast of England recently

01:38:00.450 --> 01:38:02.427
have held a webinar with the CQC

01:38:02.427 --> 01:38:05.968
and the people that oversees
the triage programme

01:38:05.968 --> 01:38:07.770
that we have

01:38:07.770 --> 01:38:10.230
to bring together and
share that best practise

01:38:10.230 --> 01:38:12.300
and those insights from the CQC.

01:38:12.300 --> 01:38:13.410
I think one of the other things

01:38:13.410 --> 01:38:16.920
that's clearly come out of CQC
inspections is the workforce.

01:38:16.920 --> 01:38:19.260
And we've talked many times to the board

01:38:19.260 --> 01:38:21.720
about the importance of the workforce

01:38:21.720 --> 01:38:24.930
within maternity and neonatal services,

01:38:24.930 --> 01:38:26.730
and I think it's really,

01:38:26.730 --> 01:38:30.210
we clearly still have variation
between organisations,

01:38:30.210 --> 01:38:33.330
both in establishments and
the numbers of midwives,

01:38:33.330 --> 01:38:36.450
obstetricians, neonatal
nurses, et cetera in post.

01:38:36.450 --> 01:38:38.340
But I think it's really important,

01:38:38.340 --> 01:38:39.270
you'll see in the paper,

01:38:39.270 --> 01:38:43.740
there are some good signs,
cautious, good signs,

01:38:43.740 --> 01:38:47.490
in some of the workforce
data that we are seeing.

01:38:47.490 --> 01:38:50.070
And just to provide a few
examples of that really,

01:38:50.070 --> 01:38:54.480
we've got 600 more midwives in post

01:38:54.480 --> 01:38:57.420
than we did this time last year.

01:38:57.420 --> 01:39:02.420
We have also got increased
numbers of midwives in training

01:39:02.790 --> 01:39:04.380
and we started to see last year

01:39:04.380 --> 01:39:08.040
the benefit of that coming
through have all been well.

01:39:08.040 --> 01:39:13.040
We expect about 1400 midwives
to enter the workforce,

01:39:13.050 --> 01:39:16.380
newly qualified around about now,

01:39:16.380 --> 01:39:20.160
and that's 400 more than we
had entering the workforce

01:39:20.160 --> 01:39:21.090
this time last year.

01:39:21.090 --> 01:39:24.120
So provided that happens and all goes well

01:39:24.120 --> 01:39:27.390
and obviously the work
that Kate talked about

01:39:27.390 --> 01:39:30.180
in supporting students is
really important in this.

01:39:30.180 --> 01:39:31.013
If that goes well,

01:39:31.013 --> 01:39:35.190
we will hit a record number
of midwives in our services

01:39:35.190 --> 01:39:37.530
by around November this year.

01:39:37.530 --> 01:39:39.180
Again, not complacent on this,

01:39:39.180 --> 01:39:41.820
we've also got programmes of
work continuing to run around

01:39:41.820 --> 01:39:43.530
international recruitment of midwives.

01:39:43.530 --> 01:39:47.430
We've had over 760 of
those now start with us

01:39:47.430 --> 01:39:51.000
in our services and
another 200 or so to start

01:39:51.000 --> 01:39:52.650
by the end of December.

01:39:52.650 --> 01:39:54.210
And great work still going on.

01:39:54.210 --> 01:39:58.263
I can see actually we've
recruited 50 more alone in August.

01:39:59.730 --> 01:40:02.250
And obviously neonatal
units have taken advantage

01:40:02.250 --> 01:40:04.080
of the nursing programme that we've run

01:40:04.080 --> 01:40:06.330
to recruit international nurses.

01:40:06.330 --> 01:40:07.980
We've had 60 more midwives start

01:40:07.980 --> 01:40:10.500
through our return to midwifery programme

01:40:10.500 --> 01:40:12.540
and great to see actually record numbers

01:40:12.540 --> 01:40:15.840
of maternity support
workers in our services.

01:40:15.840 --> 01:40:17.280
And that's important when we think about

01:40:17.280 --> 01:40:19.098
the long-term workforce plan

01:40:19.098 --> 01:40:22.890
and supporting people into
future apprenticeship roles

01:40:22.890 --> 01:40:24.498
as well.

01:40:24.498 --> 01:40:26.070
And we've also seen,

01:40:26.070 --> 01:40:27.570
I'm picking up the point Julian said

01:40:27.570 --> 01:40:28.920
about temporary staff earlier.

01:40:28.920 --> 01:40:31.398
We have seen an increase
actually in our temporary staff

01:40:31.398 --> 01:40:34.440
workforce within our maternity services,

01:40:34.440 --> 01:40:37.080
which you can see therefore the fact

01:40:37.080 --> 01:40:38.430
that we've increased the establishments,

01:40:38.430 --> 01:40:41.760
people are able to increase
the workforce overall

01:40:41.760 --> 01:40:42.873
in those areas.

01:40:44.640 --> 01:40:47.760
But we are continuing
to support our services

01:40:47.760 --> 01:40:49.590
with programmes of work.

01:40:49.590 --> 01:40:51.930
We've offered out the direct support offer

01:40:51.930 --> 01:40:54.030
to a hundred percent of our trusts.

01:40:54.030 --> 01:40:57.240
We've had 70 of those
organisations go through that now

01:40:57.240 --> 01:40:59.910
and our next phase to go through

01:40:59.910 --> 01:41:01.200
over the next couple of months.

01:41:01.200 --> 01:41:02.745
And that's really important

01:41:02.745 --> 01:41:03.780
because that's bringing trusts together

01:41:03.780 --> 01:41:05.580
to share best practise around recruitment,

01:41:05.580 --> 01:41:08.370
retention and other areas.

01:41:08.370 --> 01:41:12.390
So some really good signs here
coming through on workforce

01:41:12.390 --> 01:41:14.430
and we've obviously seen
some increases as well

01:41:14.430 --> 01:41:17.640
in the numbers of obstetricians.

01:41:17.640 --> 01:41:21.180
And the last thing I'd just
say on that is that we've got,

01:41:21.180 --> 01:41:24.120
we are also seeing
local systems continuing

01:41:24.120 --> 01:41:26.550
to invest in their workforce.

01:41:26.550 --> 01:41:28.500
We know we've had a gap
around establishment

01:41:28.500 --> 01:41:30.060
and we've supported nationally,

01:41:30.060 --> 01:41:33.690
for example, 1200 additional midwives,

01:41:33.690 --> 01:41:36.120
but we've also seen local systems invest

01:41:36.120 --> 01:41:39.360
about 900 additional midwives as well.

