Did you want to quickly say?

01:49:50.070 --> 01:49:50.903
- Yeah.

01:49:50.903 --> 01:49:52.830
Part of that leadership
programme that I talked about

01:49:52.830 --> 01:49:56.130
is helping units
understand their own staff,

01:49:56.130 --> 01:49:59.280
but we've been having
multiple listening events

01:49:59.280 --> 01:50:01.380
and equally at the regions,

01:50:01.380 --> 01:50:03.195
they're very much listening to students

01:50:03.195 --> 01:50:05.370
and trainees and what they're saying.

01:50:05.370 --> 01:50:07.710
When you look at how a service is doing,

01:50:07.710 --> 01:50:09.240
it's a very complicated picture

01:50:09.240 --> 01:50:10.560
where you need to weave together

01:50:10.560 --> 01:50:12.780
the threads of multiple elements.

01:50:12.780 --> 01:50:15.900
It's not just about outcome
data, it's what the staff say.

01:50:15.900 --> 01:50:18.360
It's what the feedback
you get from parents.

01:50:18.360 --> 01:50:21.390
It's how organisations
respond to initiatives

01:50:21.390 --> 01:50:24.060
like the NST or Saving Babies' Lives.

01:50:24.060 --> 01:50:26.730
So it is complex, Richard,

01:50:26.730 --> 01:50:27.900
an answer to your question,

01:50:27.900 --> 01:50:29.828
but we are trying our best to simplify.

01:50:29.828 --> 01:50:31.230
- Very good, Kate. Thank you.

01:50:31.230 --> 01:50:32.880
Look, I'm going to move us on and just,

01:50:32.880 --> 01:50:35.460
this is being live streamed
but also we've got some guests,

01:50:35.460 --> 01:50:37.067
members of the public guests.

01:50:37.067 --> 01:50:39.060
Just to forewarn you, I'm
afraid I'm going to lose,

01:50:39.060 --> 01:50:42.090
we're probably going to go
five minutes over the end time

01:50:42.090 --> 01:50:44.550
just to forewarn you but with apologies.

01:50:44.550 --> 01:50:47.010
So let's go to the last substantive item,

01:50:47.010 --> 01:50:50.940
which is urgent and emergency
care services. Sarah-Jane?

01:50:50.940 --> 01:50:52.620
- Thank you Chair, and
I'll keep it quite concise.

01:50:52.620 --> 01:50:53.670
Hopefully the paper's clear

01:50:53.670 --> 01:50:55.440
and I know we'll want a
bit of time for discussion.

01:50:55.440 --> 01:50:57.960
So just a few headlines to refresh people.

01:50:57.960 --> 01:50:59.820
We published the UEC Recovery Plan

01:50:59.820 --> 01:51:01.950
in January of this year.

01:51:01.950 --> 01:51:04.560
The main focus of the UEC Recovery Plan

01:51:04.560 --> 01:51:08.670
was improving our ambulance response times

01:51:08.670 --> 01:51:10.890
and our emergency department performance.

01:51:10.890 --> 01:51:12.330
But we recognised to do that

01:51:12.330 --> 01:51:14.580
what we really need to achieve is flow

01:51:14.580 --> 01:51:17.820
throughout our urgent and
emergency care pathways.

01:51:17.820 --> 01:51:20.070
And the plan set out five key ways

01:51:20.070 --> 01:51:21.510
that we were going about doing that.

01:51:21.510 --> 01:51:23.520
One was increasing capacity,

01:51:23.520 --> 01:51:25.620
one was about increasing size of workforce

01:51:25.620 --> 01:51:27.660
and the way that people work.

01:51:27.660 --> 01:51:29.490
The third was improving discharge.

01:51:29.490 --> 01:51:31.440
The fourth was care outside hospital,

01:51:31.440 --> 01:51:34.980
particularly services that
helped to not admit patients

01:51:34.980 --> 01:51:36.330
in the first place.

01:51:36.330 --> 01:51:37.920
And then the fifth was making it easier

01:51:37.920 --> 01:51:40.500
to navigate our healthcare systems.

01:51:40.500 --> 01:51:42.870
We've made significant inroads

01:51:42.870 --> 01:51:46.440
into delivering against those commitments.

01:51:46.440 --> 01:51:50.310
So in August of this year,
compared to August of last year,

01:51:50.310 --> 01:51:51.960
we were 11 minutes faster

01:51:51.960 --> 01:51:54.840
with our category two
ambulance response times,

01:51:54.840 --> 01:51:55.830
which we know is crucial.

01:51:55.830 --> 01:51:58.710
We know it's a fundamental
bedrock of the NHS

01:51:58.710 --> 01:52:01.230
that if you are in need of emergency care,

01:52:01.230 --> 01:52:02.160
in need of an ambulance,

01:52:02.160 --> 01:52:03.990
one comes to you in a timely way.

01:52:03.990 --> 01:52:06.330
So those 11 minutes are crucial.

01:52:06.330 --> 01:52:08.580
We also saw a 1% improvement

01:52:08.580 --> 01:52:10.800
in our emergency department performance

01:52:10.800 --> 01:52:14.640
against a backdrop of a
2.4% increase in attendances

01:52:14.640 --> 01:52:18.360
and a 6.8% increase in admissions.

01:52:18.360 --> 01:52:22.472
And probably the most impressive
of the statistics I think

01:52:22.472 --> 01:52:26.280
is that in August the average
number of patients in hospital

01:52:26.280 --> 01:52:28.530
that remained for 14 days or more

01:52:28.530 --> 01:52:32.520
was down by 13% on where it
was in January of this year.

01:52:32.520 --> 01:52:35.200
And we know that if we can
keep our length of stay

01:52:36.300 --> 01:52:38.910
under control, if we can
get that to come down,

01:52:38.910 --> 01:52:40.950
that really is a marker
of flow and ability

01:52:40.950 --> 01:52:43.950
to be able to admit
patients through pathways.

01:52:43.950 --> 01:52:45.630
So we are in a better position

01:52:45.630 --> 01:52:46.647
than we were this time last year.

01:52:46.647 --> 01:52:48.873
And that is as a result
of the fantastic work

01:52:48.873 --> 01:52:52.440
that the NHS has done to
implement the UEC Recovery Plan.

01:52:52.440 --> 01:52:55.530
We are also on track in terms
of the headline admissions,

01:52:55.530 --> 01:52:59.070
in particular 5,000 additional G&amp;A beds

01:52:59.070 --> 01:53:01.290
and 10,000 virtual ward beds.

01:53:01.290 --> 01:53:02.610
And we've been doing some brilliant work

01:53:02.610 --> 01:53:05.400
with our colleagues in social care

01:53:05.400 --> 01:53:07.050
on the transfer of care hubs

01:53:07.050 --> 01:53:09.840
to make sure that every
patient living in hospital

01:53:09.840 --> 01:53:12.240
with a set of more complex needs,

01:53:12.240 --> 01:53:15.930
gets a proper assessment and
gets the best pathway for them.

01:53:15.930 --> 01:53:18.780
We've published our
intermediate care framework,

01:53:18.780 --> 01:53:21.210
which we discussed in our
board deep dive yesterday

01:53:21.210 --> 01:53:22.680
in some detail.

01:53:22.680 --> 01:53:24.270
And we've been working with all systems

01:53:24.270 --> 01:53:26.460
on the 10 high impact interventions

01:53:26.460 --> 01:53:28.680
that were set out in the winter letter

01:53:28.680 --> 01:53:30.660
to make sure that our
systems have got support

01:53:30.660 --> 01:53:33.330
to do the right combination for them.

01:53:33.330 --> 01:53:34.680
So just to conclude,

01:53:34.680 --> 01:53:36.540
we have also been working then

01:53:36.540 --> 01:53:38.610
on the winter plan planning element.

01:53:38.610 --> 01:53:42.150
Clearly the UEC Recovery Plan
is for 12 months of the year,

01:53:42.150 --> 01:53:44.400
but we know we need to be really tight

01:53:44.400 --> 01:53:46.140
in the way that we approach this winter.

01:53:46.140 --> 01:53:47.970
So we've been working
with each of our systems

01:53:47.970 --> 01:53:48.803
on their plans,

01:53:48.803 --> 01:53:50.550
which we've now got back,

01:53:50.550 --> 01:53:52.440
and we've had a particular focus on those

01:53:52.440 --> 01:53:55.830
where there are gaps in
capacity and challenges.

01:53:55.830 --> 01:53:59.010
In general, we have seen
a robust response to that.

01:53:59.010 --> 01:54:01.770
Each of our systems have got plans

01:54:01.770 --> 01:54:04.260
to have the capacity in
place across the pathway

01:54:04.260 --> 01:54:07.500
to meet the expected demands over winter.

01:54:07.500 --> 01:54:09.780
However, there are some risks.

01:54:09.780 --> 01:54:12.570
While that is generically
true across the NHS,

01:54:12.570 --> 01:54:14.850
there are some individual systems

01:54:14.850 --> 01:54:18.360
that for a range, often
of historic reasons,

01:54:18.360 --> 01:54:21.270
are not as resilient to some others.

01:54:21.270 --> 01:54:23.370
So they do remain a
small number of systems

01:54:23.370 --> 01:54:26.190
that we are concerned about
and continuing to do work with.

01:54:26.190 --> 01:54:28.450
They're predominantly
those in our tier one

01:54:29.370 --> 01:54:31.140
that we work with closely anyway.

01:54:31.140 --> 01:54:32.040
We discussed earlier,

01:54:32.040 --> 01:54:34.110
but I think it's important
just to say again

01:54:34.110 --> 01:54:35.280
that industrial action,

01:54:35.280 --> 01:54:37.890
both in terms of operational impact,

01:54:37.890 --> 01:54:39.900
we will see from the,

01:54:39.900 --> 01:54:41.910
they're not finalised
figures for September yet,

01:54:41.910 --> 01:54:45.180
but we will see our emergency
performance in September

01:54:45.180 --> 01:54:49.530
was impacted by the periods
of industrial action.

01:54:49.530 --> 01:54:52.020
And we will also see as people

01:54:52.020 --> 01:54:53.850
are putting that additional
capacity in place

01:54:53.850 --> 01:54:54.960
in the lead up to winter

01:54:54.960 --> 01:54:57.480
that the interactions with
the financial challenges

01:54:57.480 --> 01:54:59.550
are very much in the forefront.

01:54:59.550 --> 01:55:02.018
And then whilst we are talking about

01:55:02.018 --> 01:55:04.680
the NHS financial situation here,

01:55:04.680 --> 01:55:05.940
the resilience of the plans

01:55:05.940 --> 01:55:09.810
also depends on social
care capacity as well.

01:55:09.810 --> 01:55:12.660
And whilst we have seen
significant increases

01:55:12.660 --> 01:55:15.270
in social care capacity
through the course of the year,

01:55:15.270 --> 01:55:16.590
and that is one of the drivers

01:55:16.590 --> 01:55:21.590
of that really impressive
statistic of the 13% reduction,

01:55:22.680 --> 01:55:24.450
there are some local
authorities, as we know,

01:55:24.450 --> 01:55:25.657
that have got a range of challenges

01:55:25.657 --> 01:55:28.560
and there are some individual places

01:55:28.560 --> 01:55:32.400
where some of that additional
capacity is now at risk.

01:55:32.400 --> 01:55:34.560
So I think we just need
to note that as well.

01:55:34.560 --> 01:55:37.140
So, pleased with where we've got to,

01:55:37.140 --> 01:55:39.990
we've got robust plans
going into this winter,

01:55:39.990 --> 01:55:43.950
but there's definitely residual
risk in a number of places

01:55:43.950 --> 01:55:46.050
and then sort of more generically driven

01:55:46.050 --> 01:55:48.030
by industrial action,

01:55:48.030 --> 01:55:50.310
and then some of the financial constraints

01:55:50.310 --> 01:55:53.223
that the NHS and social
care are operating under.

01:55:54.870 --> 01:55:58.203
- Thank you. Questions or
comments for Sarah-Jane?

01:56:01.929 --> 01:56:05.310
Mary first and then Wol.

01:56:05.310 --> 01:56:06.180
- I was just going to say

01:56:06.180 --> 01:56:08.460
that I think it's a really
comprehensive report

01:56:08.460 --> 01:56:11.280
and shows that we've
done everything we can

01:56:11.280 --> 01:56:14.610
to focus on the issues
that you've outlined,

01:56:14.610 --> 01:56:17.880
but acknowledging that industrial action

01:56:17.880 --> 01:56:19.200
challenges everything,

01:56:19.200 --> 01:56:21.423
particularly as we approach the winter.

01:56:23.220 --> 01:56:24.720
- Wol?
- I do agree with that.

01:56:24.720 --> 01:56:29.720
Just a question on the data
and the feedback loop on,

01:56:29.940 --> 01:56:32.040
in sort of real time
as to what's going on.

01:56:32.040 --> 01:56:33.597
Can you just comment
a bit more about that?

01:56:33.597 --> 01:56:35.340
Because I know you've improved that a lot

01:56:35.340 --> 01:56:36.720
and that's what this
whole thing depends on

01:56:36.720 --> 01:56:39.240
and that was, should we say,

01:56:39.240 --> 01:56:41.520
something we needed to
improve on dramatically?

01:56:41.520 --> 01:56:44.010
Have needed to improve
on dramatically in time?

01:56:44.010 --> 01:56:46.830
- So at every level there's
been massive improvements

01:56:46.830 --> 01:56:48.030
I think over the last 12 months.

01:56:48.030 --> 01:56:51.570
So the first thing is we've
got more use of data management

01:56:51.570 --> 01:56:53.460
inside organisations themselves.

01:56:53.460 --> 01:56:54.690
We've got our flagship projects

01:56:54.690 --> 01:56:56.070
around electronic bed management,

01:56:56.070 --> 01:56:58.470
but really making sure
that sort of site teams

01:56:58.470 --> 01:57:00.150
have got that clear line of sight.

01:57:00.150 --> 01:57:01.950
We've talked about the
transfer of care hub,

01:57:01.950 --> 01:57:03.630
that's a key part of that as well.

01:57:03.630 --> 01:57:06.000
Then we've got our system control centres

01:57:06.000 --> 01:57:07.590
so that there's an overview

01:57:07.590 --> 01:57:11.310
of all of the different
parts of capacity in a system

01:57:11.310 --> 01:57:13.680
and people are having those conversations

01:57:13.680 --> 01:57:17.610
about clinical risk and the
best ways to move patients

01:57:17.610 --> 01:57:20.310
and resources across particular
parts of the pathway.

01:57:20.310 --> 01:57:22.620
There's some brilliant work
being done on data sharing,

01:57:22.620 --> 01:57:25.920
some great examples of what
we can start to achieve

01:57:25.920 --> 01:57:27.780
through that shared view.

01:57:27.780 --> 01:57:29.640
And then we've also got really tight

01:57:29.640 --> 01:57:33.480
operational management
oversight in NHS England

01:57:33.480 --> 01:57:36.870
via both our regional and national teams.

01:57:36.870 --> 01:57:40.890
So we have at least a daily
check-in on real time data.

01:57:40.890 --> 01:57:44.220
We are looking, at that
particular moment in time,

01:57:44.220 --> 01:57:45.570
where have we got ambulances

01:57:45.570 --> 01:57:46.620
that have been queuing too long?

01:57:46.620 --> 01:57:47.460
Where have we got people

01:57:47.460 --> 01:57:49.200
who've been in emergency departments?

01:57:49.200 --> 01:57:52.590
Where have we got a lack
of flow and discharge?

01:57:52.590 --> 01:57:54.450
If there are particular issues,

01:57:54.450 --> 01:57:56.160
we do that at 11 o'clock every day,

01:57:56.160 --> 01:57:58.080
seven days a week in the winter.

01:57:58.080 --> 01:58:01.050
Then we can reconvene that
at a particular point in time

01:58:01.050 --> 01:58:03.180
or with a particular system.

01:58:03.180 --> 01:58:04.680
And our job as NHS England then

01:58:04.680 --> 01:58:06.510
is to get alongside those places

01:58:06.510 --> 01:58:08.460
and just help and support
with our expertise

01:58:08.460 --> 01:58:11.160
to see whether there's
anything that we can unblock.

01:58:11.160 --> 01:58:14.850
And increasingly we've been
using our more senior clinicians

01:58:14.850 --> 01:58:16.470
as part of that as well

01:58:16.470 --> 01:58:18.870
and actually asking
some of the escalations

01:58:18.870 --> 01:58:21.060
to be the clinical to clinical escalation.

01:58:21.060 --> 01:58:22.170
Because we know really,

01:58:22.170 --> 01:58:23.910
and that comes back to industrial action,

01:58:23.910 --> 01:58:25.410
when things really silt up,

01:58:25.410 --> 01:58:27.690
it is about getting those
senior decision makers

01:58:27.690 --> 01:58:31.530
on the ground able to
discharge patients and so on.

01:58:31.530 --> 01:58:33.600
So it is intense,

01:58:33.600 --> 01:58:35.250
and it's been particularly intense

01:58:35.250 --> 01:58:36.840
for those teams during industrial action

01:58:36.840 --> 01:58:39.780
because they actually do that
on a three hourly rhythm.

01:58:39.780 --> 01:58:41.640
Because clearly 24 hours

01:58:41.640 --> 01:58:42.870
is too long between that.

01:58:42.870 --> 01:58:44.490
But that's our responsibility

01:58:44.490 --> 01:58:46.020
and that's what we set up to do

01:58:46.020 --> 01:58:48.720
through the operations
centre as we go into winter.

01:58:48.720 --> 01:58:52.320
- So I'm going to ask Jim if
he has any additional comments.

01:58:52.320 --> 01:58:56.580
Just that, I call that, triage point.

01:58:56.580 --> 01:58:59.550
Front door triage point is
often quite a good intervention

01:58:59.550 --> 01:59:01.890
in terms of, when I've gone
around various systems,

01:59:01.890 --> 01:59:06.210
to see those who will put,
say, GPs at the front door.

01:59:06.210 --> 01:59:09.420
Or more senior clinicians
at the front door

01:59:09.420 --> 01:59:11.400
in times of great stress.

01:59:11.400 --> 01:59:14.130
I mean that seems to have
a real effectiveness,

01:59:14.130 --> 01:59:16.260
and I always have to mention it,

01:59:16.260 --> 01:59:21.260
but I have been told I'm
completely wrong on both of these.

01:59:21.330 --> 01:59:24.030
I always want to know about
fast response vehicles

01:59:24.030 --> 01:59:25.500
as proportions of ambulance fleets.

01:59:25.500 --> 01:59:27.090
But we can pick it up outside the board.

01:59:27.090 --> 01:59:29.220
I still can't quite understand the logic

01:59:29.220 --> 01:59:32.370
about why we wouldn't
have more fast response

01:59:32.370 --> 01:59:35.010
because the proportions of each ambulance

01:59:35.010 --> 01:59:37.770
and fast response are
very, very different.

01:59:37.770 --> 01:59:40.560
And I don't understand the logic of that.

01:59:40.560 --> 01:59:42.270
And the other one I'm always puzzled by

01:59:42.270 --> 01:59:46.410
is when we see ambulances
stacked up, actually why,

01:59:46.410 --> 01:59:49.950
again, we won't move ambulances to nearby.

01:59:49.950 --> 01:59:51.150
So it's not necessarily in rural,

01:59:51.150 --> 01:59:56.150
but nearby hospitals within
similar urban configurations.

01:59:56.250 --> 01:59:59.310
And I'm told it's because
actually once checked in,

01:59:59.310 --> 02:00:02.520
the risk of moving the
patient is more difficult.

02:00:02.520 --> 02:00:05.040
But that rather than waiting
in the background ambulance

02:00:05.040 --> 02:00:06.360
for six or seven or eight hours,

02:00:06.360 --> 02:00:10.201
if there's a nearby hospital
where there's an easier access.

02:00:10.201 --> 02:00:11.640
Don't answer them now.

02:00:11.640 --> 02:00:15.060
But, as you know, I keep
banging on at those two points.

02:00:15.060 --> 02:00:15.893
But anyway, Jim,

02:00:15.893 --> 02:00:17.520
have you got any additional comments

02:00:17.520 --> 02:00:20.010
you'd like to make to
close out the conversation?

02:00:20.010 --> 02:00:21.360
- I think the last few weeks have been

02:00:21.360 --> 02:00:23.746
a bit of a limbering on
this in terms of support

02:00:23.746 --> 02:00:26.280
for people during industrial action.

02:00:26.280 --> 02:00:29.277
So I think that's been incredibly helpful

02:00:29.277 --> 02:00:31.590
for some very challenged places

02:00:31.590 --> 02:00:33.360
and just really emphasise the value

02:00:33.360 --> 02:00:35.430
of the clinical interaction there as well,

02:00:35.430 --> 02:00:36.990
which has been hugely beneficial

02:00:36.990 --> 02:00:38.400
in the elective programme as well.

02:00:38.400 --> 02:00:42.063
And mobilising it in urgent
care has been excellent to see.

02:00:43.350 --> 02:00:44.183
- Very good.

02:00:44.183 --> 02:00:46.407
Sarah-Jane, thank you for
all you're doing in this

02:00:46.407 --> 02:00:47.957
and for all the teams actually.

02:00:48.960 --> 02:00:51.630
Thank you very much. That's
the last substantive item.

02:00:51.630 --> 02:00:54.780
I think we've got just two items to note.

02:00:54.780 --> 02:00:56.790
One is just on the Atamis,

02:00:56.790 --> 02:00:59.460
I think that's the pronunciation,
the Atamis rollout,

02:00:59.460 --> 02:01:02.400
which is an e-commercial
system for procurement.

02:01:02.400 --> 02:01:05.490
It's centrally contracted
by the Department of Health.

02:01:05.490 --> 02:01:09.390
The platform currently holds a pipeline

02:01:09.390 --> 02:01:12.480
worth 42 billion pounds
in active contracts

02:01:12.480 --> 02:01:15.240
with a combined value of over 120 billion

02:01:15.240 --> 02:01:16.950
across 11,000 suppliers.

02:01:16.950 --> 02:01:18.300
As this comes together,

02:01:18.300 --> 02:01:20.760
the ability of the NHS and
the other arms length bodies

02:01:20.760 --> 02:01:23.670
within the health system
to manage their supply base

02:01:23.670 --> 02:01:24.900
and the costs and the efficiency

02:01:24.900 --> 02:01:27.090
and effectiveness of
delivery will improve.

02:01:27.090 --> 02:01:29.820
So this is just for the
board, I think to note,

02:01:29.820 --> 02:01:32.040
and any questions or comments

02:01:32.040 --> 02:01:35.550
I think to Jacqui Rock who is
leading for us in this space.

02:01:35.550 --> 02:01:38.490
And then the last item
is simply for us to note

02:01:38.490 --> 02:01:41.790
the use of the NHS England
seal in the period,

02:01:41.790 --> 02:01:45.750
I think between 24th of March
'23 to 19th of September.

02:01:45.750 --> 02:01:48.900
And you'll see that the
seal has been applied

02:01:48.900 --> 02:01:53.550
on a number of various
programmes and authorizations.

02:01:53.550 --> 02:01:55.830
So if we note those, can I pause?

02:01:55.830 --> 02:01:57.980
Is there any other
business from the board?

02:01:59.850 --> 02:02:02.790
Can I thank you very much
again for all the engagements

02:02:02.790 --> 02:02:04.440
since the last board, I
referenced at the start,

02:02:04.440 --> 02:02:06.360
and all the committee work that you do

02:02:06.360 --> 02:02:07.740
in the two strategy boards,

02:02:07.740 --> 02:02:10.320
the board deep dive that we've had.

02:02:10.320 --> 02:02:12.330
Thank you very much and
thank you for today.

02:02:12.330 --> 02:02:13.163
Very good.

